WEBVTT

00:55.470 --> 00:56.894
Okay, and we're recording.

01:08.474 --> 01:12.194
All right, it is 4:01 on September 15th, and we

01:12.694 --> 01:16.555
will call this work session to order. I want to start off by excusing Zach.

01:16.637 --> 01:19.369
He had some other obligations today. today, Councilmember Huish.

01:20.212 --> 01:22.690
And just make sure— is Councilmember Wood online?

01:23.727 --> 01:25.866
Okay, and Councilmember Wood is also online.

01:27.155 --> 01:30.580
So glad you could join us. And, uh, we'll start right

01:31.080 --> 01:34.232
off with the first item, 1A, discussion for

01:34.878 --> 01:37.823
transmission lines. And, um,

01:38.016 --> 01:42.117
Brandon? Yeah, thank you, Mayor. Um, I think

01:42.617 --> 01:45.916
I don't need to belabor Kind of the background on this, we know

01:46.416 --> 01:50.909
there's, we know about the transmission line project

01:51.409 --> 01:53.896
that goes from Spanish Fork Canyon to the Mercer Substation.

01:54.570 --> 01:57.187
We, you can see the preferred route,

01:58.199 --> 02:01.715
even zooming in a little bit better, you can see it runs here in

02:01.780 --> 02:05.296
the yellow, and that takes us right through

02:05.424 --> 02:06.580
our ag zone.

02:09.198 --> 02:13.959
So we came to you a couple months ago to

02:14.346 --> 02:18.042
get council's feedback and see how you

02:18.542 --> 02:22.237
wanted us to handle— we've got this project, this transmission line project,

02:22.253 --> 02:25.803
we've got data centers that need power, and so we're like,

02:26.125 --> 02:29.875
how do we— how should we handle this situation?

02:29.924 --> 02:32.982
We need transmission lines to go through that area,

02:33.352 --> 02:37.434
even though transmission lines aren't allowed in the ag zone. So the—

02:39.092 --> 02:43.358
we were tasked, staff was, to prepare an overlay

02:43.858 --> 02:47.410
zone. So we were going

02:47.910 --> 02:51.648
to start working on that and then found out that Spanish Fork City

02:52.312 --> 02:56.402
had went through a similar process of actually establishing an

02:56.418 --> 03:00.083
overlay zone for the same purpose to give them

03:00.292 --> 03:03.745
control of where that transmission line Would go through

03:04.245 --> 03:08.964
their city, and then I talked. I spoke

03:09.464 --> 03:13.733
with their community development director to find out kind of some background of why they

03:14.423 --> 03:18.084
did the overlay zone, and then found out that they repealed that

03:18.180 --> 03:22.050
overlay zone or abandoned it. You know, several months later.

03:22.788 --> 03:26.899
So long story short, what it came down to was

03:27.429 --> 03:31.158
there's a. There's state requirements in the way that

03:31.449 --> 03:35.474
that really are a little bit of a challenge

03:35.491 --> 03:39.131
to work with, and so that's from what I gathered from Spanish

03:39.212 --> 03:43.326
Fork. My discussion with Spanish Fork City is they became

03:43.423 --> 03:47.771
more aware of the state code requirements related to cities

03:48.270 --> 03:52.394
and and the location permitting the and

03:52.539 --> 03:56.169
and having a say in the the location of transmission

03:56.266 --> 04:01.153
lines. And yes, there is an ability for

04:04.982 --> 04:08.297
local municipalities to have— to

04:08.797 --> 04:11.338
be able to permit these and place conditions on them.

04:12.840 --> 04:16.711
But the— it looks like one of the big keys here is

04:18.517 --> 04:21.960
providing safe, reliable, Excuse me, safe,

04:21.977 --> 04:24.274
reliable, and adequate service to customers.

04:25.046 --> 04:25.447
So,

04:28.709 --> 04:31.591
like it says here in 54-14-201,

04:36.165 --> 04:39.476
if otherwise authorized by law, local government may

04:39.976 --> 04:43.871
require or condition the construction of a facility in any manner if

04:43.920 --> 04:47.013
the requirement or condition doesn't impair the ability

04:47.513 --> 04:50.920
of the public utility to provide safe, reliable, adequate service to

04:50.937 --> 04:54.829
its customers. So we

04:55.329 --> 04:59.347
have the ability— what it really comes down

04:59.363 --> 05:03.932
to is oftentimes cities end up having to pay the

05:04.305 --> 05:09.957
actual excess cost. There's whatever—

05:10.234 --> 05:13.600
if our requirements cost

05:14.100 --> 05:17.267
the utility additional money, We have to pay for it oftentimes.

05:17.380 --> 05:21.044
Now there are some ways that that doesn't happen.

05:21.236 --> 05:25.752
There is a board, and I'll get to that in a second, but oftentimes

05:26.780 --> 05:30.010
it appears that we end up

05:30.510 --> 05:35.120
paying what the, again, the code, the state code calls actual

05:35.280 --> 05:39.442
excess cost. There's a process that cities

05:40.117 --> 05:43.619
can You can go through this

05:43.731 --> 05:48.005
process with the utility and have them give you the estimate,

05:48.069 --> 05:51.210
give you an estimate of how much it's gonna cost, and you can ask for

05:51.710 --> 05:54.058
competitive bids. But ultimately,

05:55.727 --> 05:59.082
it comes down to this. Once those

05:59.341 --> 06:04.000
actual excess costs are known and there

06:04.371 --> 06:08.376
isn't, like it says in A and B, the Utility

06:08.408 --> 06:15.653
Commission or the Utility

06:15.751 --> 06:19.850
Facility Review Board doesn't intervene in

06:19.962 --> 06:23.108
some fashion to absolve the

06:23.608 --> 06:27.040
city from having to pay, then we end up paying the excess costs.

06:27.822 --> 06:31.243
So there's your definition of actual excess cost.

06:34.303 --> 06:37.730
It can even include right-of-ways, you can see

06:38.230 --> 06:38.900
on the screen there.

06:41.940 --> 06:42.684
This chat,

06:42.765 --> 06:46.519
it's sub— or

06:47.019 --> 06:50.338
Section 2 of 5414 talks,

06:50.629 --> 06:54.012
kind of lays the groundwork for cities and

06:54.512 --> 06:57.963
how they can interact in this process of requiring

06:58.060 --> 07:01.825
conditions and so forth and permitting these transmission

07:01.941 --> 07:06.054
lines. Last, the last bit

07:06.554 --> 07:09.616
of language there, you can read that there. Any requirement or

07:10.116 --> 07:14.593
condition in any permit, authorization, approval, or exception that

07:15.093 --> 07:18.763
imposes an actual cost shall be considered waived. You basically have 30

07:18.795 --> 07:22.178
days to, if you're required

07:22.678 --> 07:26.848
to pay, you have to make that payment within 30

07:26.865 --> 07:30.857
days before the construction

07:31.357 --> 07:34.605
of the facility to avoid— anyway,

07:35.526 --> 07:38.076
if you don't pay it in a timely fashion,

07:38.381 --> 07:44.455
then your right

07:44.955 --> 07:46.447
to actually have a say actually is waived.

07:50.086 --> 07:53.671
So there is a board, like I said, the board

07:53.930 --> 07:57.598
of The Utility Facility Review Board.

07:58.261 --> 08:02.449
It is your 3 utility commissioners plus 2 other members.

08:02.546 --> 08:06.987
I think the utility company and then city gets to

08:07.487 --> 08:10.649
choose somebody from a group of people at the

08:11.149 --> 08:14.857
state government level to participate in the process.

08:15.933 --> 08:19.469
And they'll

08:19.629 --> 08:23.310
hear disputes between utilities and cities

08:23.344 --> 08:26.474
basically about you know,

08:26.763 --> 08:30.311
excess cost questions and so forth. So I

08:30.713 --> 08:34.534
just want you to be aware of this as

08:34.951 --> 08:38.002
we can prepare an overlay zone. We just

08:38.502 --> 08:40.989
want you to be aware of Spanish Fork's, uh,

08:42.209 --> 08:45.837
Spanish Fork situation, uh, kind of their

08:45.950 --> 08:49.177
experience with it, and we wanted you to be aware of the state

08:49.677 --> 08:53.118
code requirements. And so with

08:53.618 --> 08:56.277
that, I'll let you— I'm here to answer any questions.

09:02.703 --> 09:06.146
Mayor, is it okay if I ask a question? I'm just wondering,

09:06.179 --> 09:09.590
does it make any sense to the council to continue to pursue an

09:09.719 --> 09:12.985
overlay zone knowing that this is already highly regulated at the state level?

09:13.195 --> 09:16.765
I mean, it's really, in my opinion, it's a question of Do

09:17.265 --> 09:20.891
we want to impose some additional regulations and are we willing to pay

09:21.391 --> 09:25.147
for them? That's what I'm hearing. That's my interpretation of what

09:25.647 --> 09:29.324
we just read through. Is if we feel strongly that

09:29.824 --> 09:33.470
we want the power towers to

09:33.970 --> 09:38.162
be covered to look like pine trees and it costs $100,000

09:39.065 --> 09:43.104
per pole extra, are we willing to pay that? And I don't

09:43.604 --> 09:47.553
know, that's a really kind of a silly example, but as I listened

09:48.053 --> 09:50.966
to this, those were my thoughts. I don't know what the rest of you think.

09:53.192 --> 09:56.317
So part of the reason for doing the overlay zone was so that we could

09:56.817 --> 09:58.282
direct where those transmission lines would go,

09:58.492 --> 10:01.085
right?

10:02.117 --> 10:06.491
And just instead of, 'cause if we didn't wanna just put it willy-nilly and

10:06.991 --> 10:10.661
then that anywhere, the ag it could go and then we lose control

10:10.726 --> 10:14.310
of that. So that was part of the reason for the overlay. So is it

10:14.810 --> 10:17.483
placing an additional condition on the utility if we're telling it where it needs to

10:17.983 --> 10:21.623
go? Making sure I'm still on

10:21.720 --> 10:24.847
there. If it impacts, if it

10:24.879 --> 10:29.398
causes, if our condition ends

10:29.494 --> 10:33.513
up costing the utility more money, yeah, it becomes

10:34.030 --> 10:36.796
an impact to them and we have to pay pay more money. So if they

10:37.296 --> 10:41.389
can buy a transmission corridor or an easement for X number

10:41.453 --> 10:44.712
of dollars that runs right through this piece of

10:44.744 --> 10:48.197
property, and we direct that it has to go through that piece of property,

10:48.261 --> 10:51.183
and there's a cost differential between the two, we're going to have to pay that.

10:51.247 --> 10:54.154
That's, that's exactly what I'm interpreting. And remember this,

10:54.603 --> 10:57.670
they can— they're, they're a utility company, they can eminent domain anyway,

10:57.895 --> 11:01.090
even if you told them no. They They can

11:01.590 --> 11:01.624
do what they want.

11:10.419 --> 11:13.993
I— part of me is inclined to let the market help drive

11:14.106 --> 11:19.885
where the transmission lines go, because they're

11:20.385 --> 11:23.838
in the business of providing power for residents, and in the long

11:23.854 --> 11:27.534
run, if we require something extra We

11:28.034 --> 11:31.481
end up paying for it either way, whether it comes from taxpayer dollars or if

11:31.981 --> 11:35.316
it comes through ratepayers. So I think we need to be really

11:35.816 --> 11:39.423
careful and judicious about how much we are

11:39.488 --> 11:43.050
trying to control where this goes. But I also know that

11:43.082 --> 11:46.917
when we first started this conversation, there's several areas

11:46.965 --> 11:50.751
around the city that are ag-zoned, and we don't want

11:51.843 --> 11:57.621
energy utilities just running through wherever. So It

11:58.121 --> 12:02.006
feels like a lot of this is predetermined by what the state requires

12:02.182 --> 12:05.635
us to help fund if we're the ones that are instigating the

12:06.135 --> 12:10.357
cost. So it's a little bit of— it's discouraging,

12:10.373 --> 12:15.689
I think, in a lot of ways to even talk about it. But I don't

12:16.189 --> 12:19.736
know. But this relates to just the public utility. So it's not

12:19.944 --> 12:23.687
private transmission lines. So it's just the public utility.

12:23.960 --> 12:27.782
It's just public. So maybe the way the council needs to go on this is

12:27.975 --> 12:31.156
we don't put anything in code and we just wait and negotiate once an opportunity

12:31.188 --> 12:34.368
comes up. And then we can negotiate whatever we want,

12:34.384 --> 12:38.159
including saying this is how it's going to be and regard

12:38.659 --> 12:42.110
like and we can create in the code that they waive their rights to whatever

12:42.610 --> 12:45.804
the state says. That's not the way that I'm seeing it.

12:45.884 --> 12:49.176
I'm seeing that they basically. can come and secure

12:49.676 --> 12:54.085
their own access and determine where they're going to put it and where

12:54.585 --> 12:57.358
it's most cost-effective to do so. And that we really,

12:58.786 --> 13:02.219
unless we choose to step into that process and

13:02.331 --> 13:05.508
pay the cost of modifying anything

13:05.604 --> 13:08.043
that's outside of what they're already willing to do,

13:09.374 --> 13:12.663
then it's kind of a moot point. So if it's an allowed use,

13:12.679 --> 13:16.068
for instance, and it meets every requirement in

13:16.568 --> 13:19.302
an allowed use of a zone, basically staff-level approval.

13:19.885 --> 13:21.728
So it wouldn't— you would never have the chance to weigh in.

13:23.283 --> 13:27.320
Right. Well, go ahead.

13:27.384 --> 13:30.569
Let's see what Rich thinks. Rich, do you have a— Councilmember,

13:30.585 --> 13:32.954
what are you— I'm here.

13:34.968 --> 13:37.273
What I'm thinking of is— I can't hear you, Rich. I can see you talking,

13:37.322 --> 13:37.403
though.

13:43.156 --> 13:44.254
How about now? Can you hear me now?

13:48.060 --> 13:51.031
It's not muted. No. We'll come back to you.

13:53.176 --> 13:54.662
Um, did you have something else you wanted to say?

13:57.459 --> 14:00.840
I was just going to mention that I feel like part of our

14:00.857 --> 14:04.045
issue too is that we're surrounded by a lot of

14:04.238 --> 14:07.855
unincorporated land or land that belongs in a different

14:07.872 --> 14:10.892
city. So, however this gets to us,

14:12.276 --> 14:16.282
that will always play a role in it. And maybe that's why the state has

14:16.459 --> 14:19.948
played a bigger role in determining what can

14:20.448 --> 14:23.802
happen in this space. So, it's just a factor

14:23.834 --> 14:25.146
that we always have to be aware of.

14:27.076 --> 14:30.719
How about now? Can you hear me now? Is the Merkur

14:31.219 --> 14:34.458
substation the only one that is looking at upgrades? I mean,

14:34.813 --> 14:37.361
they've talked about a transmission line that might come from the Twila Valley.

14:38.565 --> 14:41.191
Are there others that we're aware of? None others.

14:47.267 --> 14:49.959
I mean, to me it just doesn't seem like this is something that we want

14:50.459 --> 14:52.386
to battle. I don't know how the rest of council feels.

14:54.772 --> 14:58.381
If there was something meaningful that we wanted to do and we were

14:58.881 --> 15:01.926
willing to pay for it, then certainly we could find a path forward there.

15:02.264 --> 15:05.805
But from my perspective, I think if the public

15:06.305 --> 15:09.821
utility company— I want to be clear— the public utility company determines that this is

15:09.837 --> 15:13.982
where the line should be, this is the most cost-effective route, and this

15:14.094 --> 15:16.793
is what they're going to do to put it in, why do we want to

15:16.825 --> 15:19.621
stand in the way of that? I mean, we want them to bring the power

15:20.121 --> 15:23.814
to our valley. That's the other consideration that we need to at least

15:24.314 --> 15:28.553
recognize is if they choose not to bring power to our valley, then that's

15:29.053 --> 15:33.259
going to limit our ability to But

15:33.759 --> 15:36.805
at least we're vetting this. At least we're talking about it. At least we didn't

15:37.305 --> 15:40.205
just have something happen without us trying to think in advance. So,

15:40.221 --> 15:42.954
like, we're trying to still play from the front, but what we're realizing is we

15:43.454 --> 15:44.987
don't have very— we don't hold the cards.

15:47.317 --> 15:50.727
You hold the checkbook is what you hold. That's how I see it.

15:50.775 --> 15:53.703
I mean, you have the checkbook that says if you're willing to pay for it,

15:53.751 --> 15:56.196
you can certainly regulate it in any way that you'd like to, but you're going

15:56.696 --> 15:58.140
to pay for it. pay for it, and you got to do it within 30

15:58.156 --> 16:01.888
days of receiving notice, unless I misunderstood

16:01.904 --> 16:05.301
something. That's how I understand it as well. I mean,

16:05.317 --> 16:08.582
again, there is this Utility Facility Review Board,

16:09.404 --> 16:12.319
um, if I keep forgetting the name of that, uh,

16:13.511 --> 16:16.909
yes, Utility Facility Review Board, that, uh,

16:17.280 --> 16:21.896
can, uh, we, we can take an issue, a dis— some

16:21.912 --> 16:25.786
type of disagreement, and have them rule on it. So there is actually

16:26.286 --> 16:30.020
a process And a possibility

16:31.330 --> 16:35.033
of this board, and the commission as

16:35.227 --> 16:39.153
well, they can take actions that may lead to the utility

16:42.654 --> 16:46.284
not imposing some of the regulations, or some

16:46.784 --> 16:49.085
of the costs, I think is a better thing to say, some of the costs

16:49.585 --> 16:53.122
that would typically come. So there's kind of a built-in review and appeal process

16:53.622 --> 16:57.434
at the state level already. But there's not much— I

16:57.934 --> 17:00.585
mean, we can't rely on that. Right,

17:00.795 --> 17:04.269
exactly. They're not— it's not a good backstop. So we're still not

17:04.301 --> 17:07.507
required to put transmission lines as

17:07.619 --> 17:11.083
an approved use in ag. We just wait basically for the utility company

17:11.583 --> 17:16.673
to come and tell us where they're going to go. Yeah, I— well,

17:17.027 --> 17:19.910
again, yeah, I guess if we say otherwise,

17:20.104 --> 17:23.436
it's just going to cost the city money, it looks like. It seems like

17:23.805 --> 17:27.274
we can still open a dialogue with them though and say, hey, here's some preferred

17:28.062 --> 17:31.065
routes we would really like you to go if possible.

17:31.370 --> 17:35.354
Yeah, and we have that ability. Commissioner— or excuse

17:35.386 --> 17:39.481
me, Councilor Wright has been in our

17:39.981 --> 17:44.123
DRC meetings, and they have Rocky Mountain Power and their consultants

17:44.623 --> 17:48.637
have come in the last few months to our DRC

17:49.137 --> 17:53.016
meeting to talk about the transmission line and getting permits

17:53.064 --> 17:58.204
and so forth. So we can continue to ask

17:58.704 --> 18:02.822
for them to adjust their application in a way

18:03.322 --> 18:06.777
that'd be favorable to the city. And we, of course,

18:06.842 --> 18:10.803
have a council member on there who's well aware of what

18:11.303 --> 18:14.797
happens on the DRC. All right. For now, while they're working on this other issue,

18:14.861 --> 18:18.382
I'm going to let Councilmember Wood weigh in on the phone. Can you hear

18:18.882 --> 18:22.318
us okay, Councilmember Wood? Yeah, can you hear me? Yeah, go ahead.

18:24.182 --> 18:27.803
So, the couple questions that I have, first of all, is this not covered under

18:28.303 --> 18:32.409
our overlay zone for the energy production overlay zone? And why

18:32.909 --> 18:35.837
not just add it to the zone that we've already created? That's my first question.

18:37.034 --> 18:39.881
The second question is, I think that we do at least want to have some

18:39.946 --> 18:44.934
input as to the— how the

18:45.434 --> 18:48.659
line comes into the city. I mean, obviously, it's got to follow a certain connection

18:49.159 --> 18:53.214
route. Um, but we— I think we want to have some input

18:53.714 --> 18:54.748
in what that looks like. They do that.

18:57.934 --> 19:02.020
Yeah, um, we want to— towers or things like that,

19:02.100 --> 19:05.253
I'm less concerned about things like that, just more or less the

19:05.317 --> 19:06.781
impact of the line itself.

19:10.981 --> 19:14.158
The alternative energy overlay zone, um,

19:14.513 --> 19:15.803
it We can certainly,

19:18.444 --> 19:22.010
we can require them to

19:22.510 --> 19:24.172
go through the rezone process.

19:26.719 --> 19:30.291
I don't have the code right in front of me. I don't recall that we

19:30.791 --> 19:34.024
have specific requirements for transmission lines in there.

19:35.155 --> 19:40.097
But I know that we also have in our 1770,

19:40.638 --> 19:43.453
Chapter 1775 of the city code,

19:44.116 --> 19:47.481
there are relatively few requirements

19:47.772 --> 19:52.331
already in there related to transmission lines.

19:53.488 --> 19:56.958
So I just don't know how much traction or how much it will help to

19:57.022 --> 20:00.369
put that land in the alternative energy

20:00.401 --> 20:04.425
overlay zone as it relates to the location

20:04.441 --> 20:09.636
of transmission lines. And your other question, I'm trying

20:09.652 --> 20:12.319
to— Can I ask a follow-up question to that, Brandon? Yeah.

20:12.513 --> 20:16.110
Okay, go ahead. So the transmission

20:16.610 --> 20:20.245
lines that we're talking about, are we talking about them coming from Spanish Fork

20:20.310 --> 20:22.378
or from the actual natural gas facility?

20:24.101 --> 20:31.858
From Spanish Fork. They'll, they'll come Can

20:32.358 --> 20:35.430
you see the screen, Rich? Is that— No, they rebooted my computer. I think they're

20:35.478 --> 20:38.648
trying to figure out how to get me connected, so I can't see anything right

20:39.148 --> 20:42.533
now. They basically start in Spanish Fork Canyon, wrap around the

20:43.033 --> 20:47.149
bottom of Utah Lake, and then cut through the

20:47.165 --> 20:51.002
backside of like the Lake Mountains and

20:51.051 --> 20:54.725
then into Cedar Valley. And is this the

20:55.225 --> 20:57.237
direct connection to the, to the current line then?

20:58.583 --> 21:02.142
Yeah, well, to the Mercer substation. Mercer substation.

21:02.351 --> 21:07.343
Okay. I don't

21:07.843 --> 21:10.882
really have any issue with the creation of a code, frankly,

21:11.318 --> 21:15.279
but I think we can probably accomplish that with the overlay that

21:15.779 --> 21:18.896
we have. I mean, I think for me at least, that was the intent of

21:19.499 --> 21:22.807
that overlay zone for all energy production, including transmission lines.

21:43.874 --> 21:47.031
I think Councilmember Wood, I mean, here's— I'm listening to what you're saying

21:47.095 --> 21:50.617
and trying to kind of process it. I think that the

21:51.021 --> 21:55.201
difference that I'm seeing is that this is regulated as a public

21:55.701 --> 21:59.060
utility by the state. And I think what you're referencing

21:59.319 --> 22:03.524
are those alternative overlay energy projects

22:04.024 --> 22:08.523
that we perhaps may entitle in

22:08.555 --> 22:12.569
the city of Eagle Mountain that are behind the meter generation

22:12.585 --> 22:17.427
facilities. And I think that's the substantive difference in in

22:17.524 --> 22:20.944
perhaps the 2 things that we're talking about. What are your thoughts about

22:21.444 --> 22:25.062
that? I'm not saying that. What I'm saying is it's coming through the same

22:25.562 --> 22:29.584
area. My understanding

22:29.600 --> 22:33.007
is they do have to at least follow existing zoning, correct?

22:35.509 --> 22:38.386
No, that's where they— I think they have the power of eminent domain,

22:39.410 --> 22:40.450
so they don't have to.

22:44.562 --> 22:47.416
So that's where we're at. What is the point of doing an overlay zone if

22:47.916 --> 22:52.177
they don't have to follow any of the zoning anyway? Well, and that's the

22:52.321 --> 22:56.553
crux of the debate. I think the question's

22:56.633 --> 23:00.352
answered then. That's exactly what they've been saying. There's no

23:00.400 --> 23:03.782
reason to do this if there's nothing we can do, right?

23:04.824 --> 23:08.656
I mean, that's— those are— I mean, I think we are coming to

23:08.688 --> 23:12.118
a consensus. That's what I'm hearing, is that if there's nothing we

23:12.618 --> 23:14.365
can do to regulate it, then why are we trying to create Create a zone

23:14.865 --> 23:17.508
that we can't regulate anyway. And if we do regulate it, it's just going to

23:18.008 --> 23:21.135
cost us money. Yeah, only pertaining to public utilities. Yes.

23:22.166 --> 23:25.651
Yeah. So, so maybe what I'm hearing is that

23:26.151 --> 23:29.636
this overlay zone would work on a behind-the-meter, but in that case,

23:31.007 --> 23:34.609
what's the impact? Do you want to do something for outside of public?

23:37.010 --> 23:40.963
Well, I, I think our alternative energy overlay zone already contemplated

23:41.463 --> 23:43.514
that, if I remember right. We'd have to go back and look at the language.

23:43.578 --> 23:47.541
I think Marcus is— it talks about transmission lines in that, in that overlay.

23:47.765 --> 23:51.246
And frankly, they're through the development agreements.

23:51.311 --> 23:54.006
That's where we've given the entitlements to produce those energy,

23:55.481 --> 23:59.331
the behind-the-meter energy production anyway. And so

23:59.831 --> 24:03.310
I think that the city still has several levers that they can pull to

24:03.422 --> 24:06.871
help design that in such a way that they're comfortable with the outcome.

24:09.677 --> 24:12.686
So are we certain that they don't want to work with us on this in

24:13.186 --> 24:13.275
the first place?

24:19.596 --> 24:24.423
They, they have been cooperative. They— I think they're—

24:26.966 --> 24:29.814
we— they came in to the DR to,

24:30.202 --> 24:33.259
to get information about submitting applications.

24:33.880 --> 24:37.157
Well, around the 1st of June, if I recall correctly, maybe May.

24:38.073 --> 24:41.544
And we told them, so when we go

24:42.044 --> 24:46.075
through that process, we just kind of give them a bunch of background information,

24:46.204 --> 24:49.819
requirements they need to go through, and then kind of tell them about constraints they

24:50.044 --> 24:53.017
may run into. And so obviously, this ag zone,

24:53.097 --> 24:56.777
the ag zone not allowing transmission

24:56.873 --> 24:59.572
lines, that was the biggest roadblock. And so they,

25:02.654 --> 25:06.008
They actually were the ones that they've gently

25:06.666 --> 25:10.133
just kind of pointed to the fact to the state code. They're like, "You also

25:10.633 --> 25:13.904
might want to talk to Spanish Fork." And yet they didn't really

25:14.404 --> 25:17.660
give me a lot of feedback initially, but they're like, "You might want to go

25:17.916 --> 25:21.399
talk to Spanish Fork because they've been down this path and hear

25:21.447 --> 25:25.251
from them what kind of their their experience with trying

25:25.299 --> 25:28.668
to create an overlay." So I did, and I'm reporting

25:28.766 --> 25:32.749
back what I heard, what I found out in my own code

25:36.750 --> 25:40.368
So— So we good to move on on this one, or is there further

25:40.868 --> 25:43.918
direction for staff? No,

25:44.063 --> 25:47.537
I think— Doesn't sound like there's anything we can do. Exactly my thought.

25:47.666 --> 25:51.205
I think we just say thanks for looking into it and bringing us this information.

25:51.938 --> 25:55.538
And that helps us make a decision that it's better off not to make

25:56.038 --> 25:59.394
a decision about this. Okay, let's roll

25:59.508 --> 26:03.149
on. I'm going to keep you on the phone for a second, Rich, until I

26:03.649 --> 26:05.788
find out if this is there, but I'm going to put it on mute.

26:06.986 --> 26:10.547
So if you need something, raise your hand or something, we'll get to you.

26:17.185 --> 26:20.302
Discussion item 1B, I think we're going to hold off on that one. I've mentioned

26:20.802 --> 26:23.941
before the meeting, Councilmember Wood, I didn't get a chance to tell you, but that

26:23.958 --> 26:27.880
was one that Council Member Hewish wanted to weigh in on, and I think we're

26:28.009 --> 26:31.463
better served to hold off on that one to maybe

26:31.963 --> 26:35.097
the next meeting. So if Lacey, if you don't mind putting that on a future

26:38.588 --> 26:41.970
It is somewhat urgent, but I don't think it's super urgent, so we'll skip

26:42.470 --> 26:45.900
by that one. And then discussion once— item 1C,

26:45.916 --> 26:49.708
the discussion about the project recommendations from the community board.

26:49.772 --> 26:52.769
Steve, did you have an introduction, or is this Turn it over to the board.

26:53.331 --> 26:56.398
Oh, we can turn it over to Jesse, who's representing the board, and Charlotte's here

26:56.898 --> 26:57.779
as well. So.

27:19.995 --> 27:23.954
Give us just one second. I think we're

27:24.454 --> 27:27.536
recording. Oh, we're good.

27:29.615 --> 27:33.177
Just not what? No sound? I thought you were asking about Rich, but Rich

27:33.677 --> 27:35.207
is not back. Yeah, we're good. I'll keep him on the phone for a minute

27:36.329 --> 27:38.849
and tell— if something changes, we'll figure it out.

27:39.109 --> 27:40.003
Okay.

27:43.564 --> 27:47.499
Good afternoon, Mayor and Council. I really appreciate

27:47.999 --> 27:51.980
the opportunity to come and present this to you today. And before I get

27:52.480 --> 27:55.304
started, I want to recognize the strain that both our staff and our budget are

27:55.353 --> 27:58.645
under right now. And the last thing I want to do is add

27:58.661 --> 28:02.885
unnecessarily to either of those, which is why we took careful consideration in

28:02.917 --> 28:06.804
crafting this list. I also want to express my gratitude for all

28:06.852 --> 28:10.140
of the help staff has already given us on the Community Services Board. And for

28:10.238 --> 28:12.733
all of the work that each of you continues to put into our city,

28:15.095 --> 28:18.797
the information that we put together here came from a few different places.

28:18.911 --> 28:22.387
We gathered it from discussing with residents at

28:22.887 --> 28:26.915
different events in the city. There was an app that was created

28:27.415 --> 28:30.085
that had customer or customer resident feedback,

28:30.942 --> 28:33.441
and then a lot of this was just things that members of the board had

28:33.458 --> 28:35.313
seen around the city.

28:37.323 --> 28:40.461
We then dedicated the majority of one of our board meetings to discussing what we

28:40.961 --> 28:44.563
believed should be priorities for the city, and we specifically

28:45.063 --> 28:48.577
tried to identify things that could be meaningful wins without necessarily taking on

28:48.853 --> 28:52.901
another massive project. And then we— from

28:52.917 --> 28:57.087
there, I did some additional research to

28:57.587 --> 29:00.935
better understand what could actually be feasible and what we could realistically

29:01.032 --> 29:04.604
ask. Thank you. This is the

29:05.104 --> 29:07.930
list that we came up with. I do want to point out that as a

29:08.058 --> 29:11.738
board, we did not rank these recommendations beyond agreeing that they belonged

29:12.238 --> 29:15.658
on this list. But I intentionally left them

29:15.867 --> 29:19.289
this way because I know that funding is tight right

29:19.789 --> 29:22.567
now and that it may not be feasible to address all of these immediately.

29:23.724 --> 29:27.001
However, as I researched each one further, I found some pretty compelling reasons why

29:27.082 --> 29:29.683
every one of them deserves consideration. Thank you. Consideration as a priority.

29:31.474 --> 29:34.847
The first one is ADA curb ramp installations.

29:35.121 --> 29:38.960
There are currently these, at least these 5 locations

29:38.992 --> 29:43.100
that were pointed out to me by a resident where the

29:44.228 --> 29:46.760
trail does not have an ADA ramp.

29:49.333 --> 29:54.019
In the staff report, Steve noted that the average cost is approximately $4,450

29:54.051 --> 29:57.849
per ramp. And that's not an insignificant amount of money.

29:58.587 --> 30:02.150
And I don't know exactly how many there will be, but I do think that

30:03.242 --> 30:06.612
they're important improvements for us to make. One of

30:07.112 --> 30:10.544
our board members made a comment during our discussion that I think summed this

30:11.044 --> 30:13.562
all up, and it's that our city should be accessible for everyone.

30:14.974 --> 30:18.938
Did you have something? Yeah, go ahead. Yeah, I do have a question. And it's,

30:19.243 --> 30:22.549
so at 44, 50 per ramp at each of these locations

30:22.661 --> 30:26.127
that the board has identified. Are— does that mean there's 4 ramps at this

30:26.627 --> 30:30.556
intersection, or is it just one? There's 5. These are 5 different locations.

30:30.861 --> 30:34.535
5 locations. But let's take Shep's Ridge and Overland Pump

30:35.035 --> 30:38.274
Track. Is it just one ADA ramp, or is it multiple ADA ramps at that

30:38.322 --> 30:41.467
location? So there would just be one at that one, and then on the other

30:41.967 --> 30:45.350
side is a different— so it would be the White Rock Road would be the

30:45.366 --> 30:48.383
other side of the trail. Each of these 5 represent one ramp apiece? Yes.

30:49.148 --> 30:52.836
Okay, I think so. Just real quick, I wanted to either get

30:52.853 --> 30:56.235
Zach or Brad to weigh in. A couple of these we had identified earlier

30:56.299 --> 30:59.830
on and might already be on some lists. Is that which is very possible?

30:59.911 --> 31:03.543
True. I know there was one in down

31:04.043 --> 31:07.513
here to the south. What's that called? Talking about Sweetwater.

31:09.830 --> 31:12.919
Sweetwater. Is this and some of them the sidewalk was

31:12.952 --> 31:15.891
meant to go 100 feet to the East or west,

31:15.987 --> 31:18.789
and then there was one. So— Well,

31:18.805 --> 31:22.236
Mayor, I think that there's already a funding source identified for these

31:22.268 --> 31:25.056
ADA ramps from the CDBG funds. And that's why I was asking.

31:25.574 --> 31:29.066
I mean, didn't we talk about that? Yeah, I think

31:29.115 --> 31:32.575
that we've had communication that that funding could be utilized

31:32.672 --> 31:36.256
for something of this nature. Well, I— yeah, so I think this one's a

31:36.305 --> 31:39.095
no-brainer. Me personally.

31:41.870 --> 31:44.534
Awesome. Can I just make one point before we move on with that though?

31:45.277 --> 31:48.863
On some of these, we need to look at them because when we build ADAs,

31:49.234 --> 31:53.048
we gotta be careful. Like, is the trail continuing through and

31:53.453 --> 31:56.364
does that create a crosswalk scenario?

31:57.237 --> 32:00.618
Is the ADA intended to be there or is there an ADA at

32:00.650 --> 32:04.026
the corner, at the intersection,

32:04.042 --> 32:07.895
and this is a cross-section road? Definitely willing

32:07.927 --> 32:11.445
to look at the list and you know assess it, but there's a few other

32:11.945 --> 32:15.123
variables I think we need to look at through that process. That's why I was

32:15.220 --> 32:18.272
wondering if there's additional feedback because I know we looked at one down here that

32:18.772 --> 32:23.412
has that exact question. So we

32:23.912 --> 32:27.011
can look further into those. All right, good. Can I ask just a quick question

32:27.123 --> 32:30.770
too, Mayor? Since we only have this one item,

32:31.991 --> 32:35.744
have we fixed the loophole? In our requirements

32:35.808 --> 32:39.722
for neighborhoods so that this stuff doesn't happen anymore?

32:40.240 --> 32:44.372
How did we get to the point where one of these trails didn't have

32:44.472 --> 32:45.320
an ADA ramp?

32:49.738 --> 32:53.399
I think that's something that we're going to have to assess on a case-by-case.

32:54.254 --> 32:57.739
I think as we develop our subdivisions and the

32:58.239 --> 33:01.055
communities, we look at those plans and we try to make sure that those cross-sections

33:01.555 --> 33:04.984
are there and the ADA ramps. exist. Um, some of these areas,

33:05.336 --> 33:09.262
uh, I'm, I'm not familiar with these intersections, so I haven't seen them,

33:09.359 --> 33:11.730
so I'll have to go out and look at them and assess them. But,

33:11.810 --> 33:15.256
uh, it's, it's definitely something that we try to collect

33:15.756 --> 33:19.006
in our DRC, our plan reviews, and try to make sure that everything's compliant and

33:19.182 --> 33:22.595
in line. I, I totally get that, and I

33:22.643 --> 33:25.913
know that you guys make great efforts to make sure all

33:26.413 --> 33:29.778
of those things happen, but I'm wondering if we need to have something in our

33:29.843 --> 33:33.070
code or in our process that would

33:33.570 --> 33:37.004
make sure that these ADA curb ramps

33:37.084 --> 33:40.938
are there, just like we require that there's X amount of streetlights

33:41.438 --> 33:44.711
and X amount of park amenities and things like that. So I'm just wondering if

33:45.211 --> 33:49.127
this is something we ask the planning department to take a

33:49.627 --> 33:53.125
look at code-wise to help—

33:53.542 --> 33:56.756
Make it always work out the right way?

33:58.860 --> 34:01.816
I mean, we can definitely look at anything and put it in code, but I

34:02.316 --> 34:05.571
think what is gonna really dictate this on how these

34:05.604 --> 34:08.848
exist and whatnot will be these future master plans

34:08.896 --> 34:12.060
with our trails and whatnot. They should outline and show

34:12.690 --> 34:16.347
that connectivity. And, you know, when it comes to ADA and

34:16.847 --> 34:20.398
sidewalk, there's other plans in place,

34:20.639 --> 34:24.447
and there's some other plans I think us as a community that we're going

34:24.947 --> 34:28.223
to probably have to get involved in because, you know, ADA is one

34:28.723 --> 34:32.305
of the biggest compliance things that we have to follow. And we're trying to

34:32.805 --> 34:36.081
get on a maintenance schedule. We're trying to add these additional ADA ramps

34:36.581 --> 34:39.680
and those things as they come up. So the best case scenario I can give

34:40.180 --> 34:43.360
you for today with this is we can assess each of these locations and

34:43.810 --> 34:46.814
see if it warrants it. And if And if we— if it needs one,

34:46.927 --> 34:50.475
or we'll do what we need to to get it installed.

34:50.764 --> 34:53.718
Thanks. Can I add just one other thing too? So if you're saying,

34:53.830 --> 34:57.844
Brad, that there might be a ramp down, like, down a

34:58.344 --> 35:01.632
ways, if the rest of the family's going this way, but they've got an ADA

35:01.761 --> 35:03.976
and they said, well, now we're gonna have to detour to bring you in,

35:04.105 --> 35:08.166
I'm still open to adding more ramps to make it better for those

35:08.198 --> 35:11.586
that are traversing. And I hear you. If it's a through

35:11.714 --> 35:15.005
cross— if it's a trail segment that's crossing a roadway, then we need

35:15.505 --> 35:18.473
to assess it in that situation. But that's not true in every scenario.

35:18.570 --> 35:20.818
Sometimes trails go to a sidewalk and they tee.

35:22.006 --> 35:24.639
And that's what I'm saying is we wouldn't want to put an ADA into the

35:25.139 --> 35:28.765
roadway on a tee. That's why it needs to be assessed.

35:28.830 --> 35:32.539
This might have an ADA ramp a couple hundred feet to the east

35:32.667 --> 35:36.071
at a stop sign. And this is mid-block where you're— now you've got a crosswalk

35:36.571 --> 35:39.635
situation or something. So we need to look at those. And then

35:39.989 --> 35:42.310
I was just going to— I think I know the answer to this. I don't

35:42.810 --> 35:47.465
remember that $4,450 was a number that you got from engineering,

35:47.482 --> 35:50.857
or is that? From streets.

35:51.325 --> 35:54.976
Okay, Steve got it from— I just—

35:55.476 --> 35:58.513
and we're pretty confident in that number, right? Yeah, that's reasonable.

36:01.131 --> 36:03.386
Okay, just so that we know the fiscal impact. Go ahead.

36:06.802 --> 36:10.136
Thank you. Our next item is trail signage.

36:10.953 --> 36:14.559
The off-street trails master plan that was recently passed

36:14.640 --> 36:19.896
identified 39 signage-related issues throughout our trail system.

36:20.169 --> 36:23.102
And some of them are a little more than just an inconvenience.

36:24.096 --> 36:28.102
There are blind intersections where ATVs and mountain bikes can meet without adequate

36:28.602 --> 36:32.093
visibility or warning. Trail difficulty isn't always

36:32.109 --> 36:35.539
marked, which means a beginner can unintentionally end up on a much more advanced trail.

36:36.212 --> 36:40.189
Boundaries aren't always clear, making it easy for someone to leave designated trail without realizing

36:40.285 --> 36:44.357
it. And in many areas, the permitted

36:44.389 --> 36:47.837
uses aren't obvious, so users don't know whether it's a trail for

36:48.337 --> 36:50.963
bikes, ATVs, horses, or anything else.

36:51.348 --> 36:55.308
Our recommendation is

36:55.453 --> 36:58.723
to create cohesive signs that

36:59.220 --> 37:02.908
address all of those issues that would improve safety and make

37:03.408 --> 37:06.647
trails easier to navigate. and create a better experience for everyone using them.

37:08.108 --> 37:10.613
I love this one because I think one of the things that we hear a

37:11.113 --> 37:14.691
lot from residents is either enforcing something that

37:14.707 --> 37:17.212
should or shouldn't be on a trail,

37:18.303 --> 37:21.900
noise from certain things that shouldn't be adjacent to

37:21.916 --> 37:26.636
backyards at 3 in the morning, just different things like that. And I think having

37:26.668 --> 37:29.093
good signage defining what is there,

37:30.683 --> 37:34.162
Both for the

37:34.662 --> 37:37.770
ability and the difficulty of a trail, but also just

37:38.270 --> 37:41.659
to help people understand what's allowed there and not is the first step

37:41.804 --> 37:45.051
into being able to enforce something down the road too.

37:45.132 --> 37:47.393
So I hope we can fund this.

37:49.843 --> 37:52.590
Is that 39, meaning there's 39 identified locations? Yeah.

37:53.495 --> 37:56.727
Okay. Just as we go on, and I actually

37:57.227 --> 38:00.563
wrote down I like this one too. Melissa said that. I just don't want it

38:01.063 --> 38:04.261
to fall on deaf ears. So as we get going, great presentation. I just want

38:04.761 --> 38:08.320
to make assignments. So keep that in mind that— It said 39

38:08.417 --> 38:11.585
issues. It could be that there are locations, but I would assume

38:12.085 --> 38:15.203
that's what that means. Mayor?

38:15.283 --> 38:19.674
Can I just ask— Can you guys hear me yet? So is that 39 pertaining

38:19.706 --> 38:22.569
to the app? Is that how that is correlated?

38:24.074 --> 38:27.712
'Cause then you collect this information— No, this is from the off-street

38:28.212 --> 38:31.785
trails master plan. This is from— okay. So there's no correlation back to the app.

38:31.914 --> 38:33.499
I just was wondering if that's what the 39 was.

38:35.839 --> 38:39.040
No. The next one we have

38:39.540 --> 38:41.087
on here is benches at Smith Ranch Park.

38:42.819 --> 38:46.318
Smith Ranch Park is an awesome park, and it has 3

38:46.818 --> 38:49.913
different playgrounds and multiple fields and cool wildlife area,

38:49.929 --> 38:52.377
and it's It's— there's a lot for families to do there.

38:54.096 --> 38:58.177
However, around those playgrounds, there's nowhere to sit. And as

38:58.933 --> 39:02.821
a mom who has taken her kids there to play, it would be really nice

39:02.950 --> 39:06.370
if there were benches or something to sit

39:06.436 --> 39:08.770
on so that I wouldn't have to bring a camp chair to watch them.

39:11.940 --> 39:15.514
It was noted in the staff report that it would be just under $1,000

39:16.014 --> 39:19.172
per bench. We also had the thought that if

39:19.672 --> 39:23.276
it were allowed, small businesses, or not necessarily

39:23.776 --> 39:26.019
small businesses, but businesses in the community could donate a bench.

39:27.616 --> 39:30.424
Because I know they're always looking for opportunities to give back to the community,

39:30.536 --> 39:33.530
and I think that would be beneficial. I don't know how many benches we would

39:34.030 --> 39:37.481
need, but at least a few around each playground

39:37.643 --> 39:41.058
would be awesome. I also really like this

39:41.558 --> 39:45.399
idea. One thing that I've heard over the years of serving from different

39:45.899 --> 39:49.336
neighborhoods is when someone from a neighborhood passes away,

39:50.203 --> 39:54.108
like a small child or something like that, almost always I

39:54.124 --> 39:57.177
have residents reach out and say, we want to do something,

39:57.273 --> 40:00.551
we'd like to donate this memorial or donate something.

40:00.664 --> 40:04.970
And I think this allows us to achieve

40:05.066 --> 40:08.344
2 things. One, provide more seating, but then also

40:08.360 --> 40:12.243
a standardized option for creating a

40:12.743 --> 40:16.138
memorial bench in a loved one's honor. And I think

40:16.638 --> 40:20.001
we could do it as a pilot program at Smith Ranch Park,

40:20.546 --> 40:23.495
and as we see how that works,

40:24.650 --> 40:28.833
we could potentially expand it to other parts of the city so

40:29.333 --> 40:33.706
that people can designate where they'd like for a memorial bench to be placed.

40:34.412 --> 40:37.935
And I like that the park staff is picking out

40:38.291 --> 40:41.945
the quality and type of bench so it's not just

40:42.025 --> 40:45.857
some kid's Eagle project, but it's actually something

40:45.889 --> 40:48.502
that will stay and last in a city park.

40:53.097 --> 40:56.435
So. The next one would be the maintenance of

40:56.935 --> 41:00.531
the rodeo grounds. This is something that I've heard from several members of the community

41:00.563 --> 41:04.880
who own and ride horses. And particularly one member of our board who

41:05.249 --> 41:08.600
this has been an issue for. Currently, the grounds are groomed

41:09.100 --> 41:12.368
and prepared like a week before the rodeo

41:12.384 --> 41:13.891
each year, within that week.

41:14.372 --> 41:17.900
And then within about a week after

41:18.400 --> 41:21.909
the rodeo, I've been told that the arena surface becomes difficult or

41:22.409 --> 41:25.757
unusable again, and it generally stays that way until preparations begin for

41:26.257 --> 41:30.022
the next rodeo. And when we're talking about maintenance, we are

41:30.522 --> 41:33.836
specifically talking about maintaining the dirt surface pictured in this

41:34.493 --> 41:37.040
to the left, not maintaining the entire facility.

41:38.338 --> 41:41.703
Um, however, during my research, I did find that the current

41:41.719 --> 41:45.132
lease agreement with Pony Express Events could create some confusion about

41:45.228 --> 41:48.881
who's responsible for certain types of maintenance, and so that may need

41:48.929 --> 41:52.102
to be reviewed. Um, but ultimately,

41:52.631 --> 41:56.316
our recommendation as a board is that the city take on the responsibility for

41:56.816 --> 42:00.703
maintaining the dirt and add it to some maintenance schedule

42:01.204 --> 42:02.723
like with the rest of the parks in the city.

42:06.690 --> 42:09.975
Our next one is more of a

42:10.040 --> 42:13.682
fun one. We are recommending wildlife-themed

42:14.182 --> 42:18.285
utility box art. This was brought to us by a member

42:18.785 --> 42:22.489
of the board who is also part of the Wildlife Alliance. And the idea

42:22.989 --> 42:25.680
is to take some of the utility boxes through the city and wrap them in

42:25.798 --> 42:29.418
artwork similar to these examples. The estimated

42:29.918 --> 42:33.378
cost is about $1,000 to $1,500 per box, and we would need to coordinate with

42:33.426 --> 42:36.228
Rocky Mountain Power to determine which boxes could be approved.

42:38.442 --> 42:41.609
And there are, as far as I understand,

42:41.657 --> 42:45.147
funds set aside from capital projects that are restricted to

42:45.647 --> 42:49.287
be used for public art, and we thought that

42:49.335 --> 42:52.927
this would be a really creative use of those funds.

42:52.991 --> 42:57.927
We also thought that it would be great to provide

42:58.427 --> 43:02.111
some community engagement for— and we could have like an art contest and

43:02.611 --> 43:05.245
decide which pieces of art could be displayed on these.

43:06.637 --> 43:10.441
And the art itself should last 5

43:10.941 --> 43:12.092
to 7 years if we wrap it in vinyl.

43:17.850 --> 43:21.515
The next one is Nolan Park. I think we all

43:21.547 --> 43:24.407
know that Noland Park has a lot of potential, but right now we're not getting

43:24.455 --> 43:27.008
everything out of it that we could.

43:28.970 --> 43:32.993
The fields are used, but the baseball diamonds aren't configured to accommodate

43:33.994 --> 43:37.291
full baseball games. The splash pad's unusable, and there is

43:37.486 --> 43:40.850
nowhere to park. Our recommendation is that the city begin

43:40.899 --> 43:44.003
the process of a redesign of the park sooner rather than later.

43:45.267 --> 43:48.847
It's intentionally lower down on the priority list because the IAE recognized

43:49.347 --> 43:53.293
that redesigning and eventually rebuilding a park is much larger and more expensive undertaking than

43:53.376 --> 43:55.151
several of the other recommendations.

43:57.975 --> 44:01.412
But it would

44:01.444 --> 44:04.881
be useful to at least start the process,

44:05.058 --> 44:08.259
gain resident feedback, and see what it is

44:08.356 --> 44:11.650
that we want to build and where— what

44:11.666 --> 44:14.639
direction we should go, if we have to do a new splash pad or not.

44:14.944 --> 44:17.583
I know that that's important to a lot of residents. I heard that.

44:18.637 --> 44:22.035
just to gain resident feedback and see what the next steps would need to

44:22.535 --> 44:26.625
be and if we need to hire a firm. But we, we believe that that's

44:27.125 --> 44:27.979
something worth considering at this time.

44:30.825 --> 44:34.365
Really quick, um, I think that this is

44:34.865 --> 44:38.066
important because it's something that's been talked about for several years,

44:38.179 --> 44:41.536
and I think we just need to rip the Band-Aid off and start

44:41.632 --> 44:45.673
working forward with a plan. If we can't pay for something

44:45.850 --> 44:48.997
for a while and we need to phase it in smaller chunks, that's okay too.

44:49.318 --> 44:52.979
The one thing that I want us to make sure we consider is

44:53.091 --> 44:56.222
Nolan Park East, which is kind of

44:56.722 --> 45:00.509
like a section not in the main square of Nolan

45:00.622 --> 45:04.235
Park, but is to the east of it. It's got a pavilion and

45:04.315 --> 45:07.799
quite a bit of grass space. South of the church there?

45:08.185 --> 45:09.549
It would be north of the church.

45:11.680 --> 45:15.103
Along there. It's along, what is that road over there,

45:15.569 --> 45:19.024
Peregrine? Yes. So it's right along Peregrine and

45:19.524 --> 45:23.091
it faces the townhomes right there. There are,

45:24.272 --> 45:28.431
when I've been there at Nolan Park East, I've seen a lot of people take

45:28.544 --> 45:32.180
their dogs from the higher density housing and

45:32.196 --> 45:35.184
go over there and let their dogs run and play in that area.

45:36.218 --> 45:40.140
We've talked about community gardens that might be a place that's

45:40.640 --> 45:44.247
near density. And I

45:44.747 --> 45:48.902
think that since we have kind of a community garden pilot program we're starting

45:48.918 --> 45:52.736
up, this would be a great second location to consider. But for sure,

45:52.768 --> 45:55.916
it's important to me that we're looking at Nolan Park

45:56.416 --> 45:59.468
holistically and not just the square, but also Nolan Park East.

46:01.010 --> 46:02.275
Thank you.

46:05.895 --> 46:09.645
The last one is a— it's about a

46:10.145 --> 46:14.292
championship-level disc golf course. And I want to emphasize what

46:14.792 --> 46:18.042
we're actually asking is not for the city

46:18.154 --> 46:21.311
to select a location or design a course

46:21.439 --> 46:25.253
or spend money constructing one. Our recommendation is to engage with

46:25.269 --> 46:28.682
Firefly and explore whether this is something that could make sense within their

46:28.730 --> 46:32.563
community. Pictured on the left is our current

46:33.701 --> 46:37.261
disc golf course, and it works for casual play, but it really isn't suitable

46:37.309 --> 46:40.837
for more serious or avid players. The space is confined

46:41.337 --> 46:44.045
enough that longer throws can end up near or in people's backyards,

46:44.846 --> 46:48.952
which is not ideal. Firefly has expressed some interest in potentially

46:49.000 --> 46:52.528
having a championship-level course there, and they have consistently looked for

46:53.028 --> 46:55.639
opportunities to bring unique amenities into their community.

46:56.641 --> 47:00.382
So our ask is just that the conversation is had with Firefly on if this

47:00.430 --> 47:04.091
is something that could— that they could be interested in,

47:04.284 --> 47:08.195
and then if they are, that you guys seriously consider allowing

47:08.227 --> 47:11.556
and supporting that opportunity. But of course,

47:11.845 --> 47:17.321
they have not committed to anything. So that

47:17.402 --> 47:21.025
is all. Thank you. I really appreciate you taking the time to hear these

47:21.122 --> 47:24.550
today, and if you have any questions. Yeah, let's— I know we're going to back

47:25.050 --> 47:27.690
up a little ways. Uh, I've got Council Member Wood on the line again,

47:28.190 --> 47:31.390
and we'll go to him for some questions. Did you want to go back to—

47:31.890 --> 47:35.230
Yeah, yeah, I just have a couple things just to address. So with

47:35.730 --> 47:39.329
the signage, I think you're right on those, but I think we need to codify

47:39.829 --> 47:44.190
this so that everything going forward has those safety standards in place.

47:44.210 --> 47:47.550
And then we need to put it on a schedule to go address that while

47:48.050 --> 47:51.630
we're efficient on all of our Trails.

47:52.130 --> 47:54.750
Trails. And again, I don't think we have the money to do everything all at

47:55.250 --> 47:58.550
the moment, but if we don't put a plan in place that addresses how we're

47:59.050 --> 48:02.070
going to pay for these things and get them in, uh, into a routine of

48:02.570 --> 48:05.650
getting updated and fixed, it's never going to

48:06.150 --> 48:09.110
happen. We'll be talking about it again in a year. The second thing,

48:09.610 --> 48:12.810
uh, I agree with the assumptions made about Miller

48:13.310 --> 48:16.930
Park, but I maybe would like to see a little different approach. I think that

48:17.430 --> 48:20.850
we have other parks that have problems as well, and we need to have—

48:21.350 --> 48:25.150
this would be something that would be a real benefit from our Community Services Board

48:25.650 --> 48:28.790
to evaluate what is the most meaningful

48:29.290 --> 48:32.540
first for each of the parks, to put that in a priority list,

48:32.970 --> 48:35.980
and then let's put a plan together to start addressing them.

48:37.090 --> 48:40.430
Because I think it's more than just Miller Park. I mean, we're aware of what

48:40.930 --> 48:43.770
the splash pad issue is. But I think that there are other parks that require

48:44.270 --> 48:48.310
the same type of, uh, to be addressed.

48:48.710 --> 48:51.930
And if we don't put it on a plan to address them, then it

48:52.430 --> 48:56.230
never gets funded. Rich, she did mention

48:56.730 --> 49:00.630
park benches at, uh, Smith Ranch was another one, and then Nolan

49:01.130 --> 49:04.790
Park. And those were the 2 main parks, but definitely

49:05.290 --> 49:08.990
had those too. I don't have an issue with putting

49:09.490 --> 49:11.730
the benches in. I think that's a good amenity. I mean, people can use them

49:12.410 --> 49:15.891
When their kids are playing, people who are walking and want to

49:16.391 --> 49:19.195
take a break, or runners, or people that just want to sit at the park.

49:19.307 --> 49:21.934
I think that's an amenity that should be in all of our parks.

49:23.882 --> 49:27.578
I just want to agree with Council Member Wood. I think that we've talked

49:28.078 --> 49:31.917
about making some upgrades to even at the Silver Lake Park where

49:32.417 --> 49:35.926
the amphitheater is and the playground and the walking trail around there.

49:36.483 --> 49:40.080
This might be a time to imagine what else

49:40.580 --> 49:44.887
could go there. We've talked about landslides that are

49:44.935 --> 49:49.245
on the side of the hill there and just different things, and maybe beefing

49:49.342 --> 49:52.947
up and adding bathrooms down by the amphitheater part

49:53.447 --> 49:56.969
so that the summer camp that takes place there every

49:57.469 --> 50:00.398
summer has an actual place for them to change in and out of costumes and

50:00.414 --> 50:03.699
just different things like that. So I like the idea of

50:03.763 --> 50:07.470
asking our Community Services Board to look at all of the parks and help

50:07.970 --> 50:10.731
prioritize some of the most urgent needs.

50:11.631 --> 50:15.230
I'm okay with that, but I'd want them to have maybe an email or

50:15.730 --> 50:19.171
something to say, hey, look here, and this is an idea, because otherwise it's just

50:19.705 --> 50:22.919
kind of throwing darts. So if it's things like the slide that I know we've

50:23.419 --> 50:26.822
talked about, I just, I want

50:27.322 --> 50:31.047
to make sure we're giving them good direction. Mayor, I think that we need to

50:31.547 --> 50:34.301
make sure that we're doing this in conjunction with our new parks manager.

50:35.780 --> 50:38.560
I'm assuming the global plan that they're going to be providing,

50:39.310 --> 50:42.510
and I think that they should be working with the Community Services Board, but I'd

50:43.010 --> 50:46.710
like to see a maintenance plan to bring the parks to a level that I

50:47.210 --> 50:50.430
think we're all comfortable with, which is going to require assessing each of the

50:50.930 --> 50:54.220
parks and where they're currently at with their landscaping,

50:54.890 --> 50:58.750
the equipment that's there, the trails, etc. I think

50:59.250 --> 51:02.770
adding what Melissa said on top of that, finishing some of the parks needs to

51:03.270 --> 51:06.740
be part of that. But if those aren't all prioritized in,

51:06.780 --> 51:09.380
you know, going to this park, here are the things that needs to happen.

51:09.880 --> 51:12.120
We go to this park, here's the things that need to happen. We need to

51:12.620 --> 51:16.430
put those into some sort of a budget plan or it will never happen.

51:16.800 --> 51:20.099
Yeah, and just probably a good time to tell you a meeting that I had

51:20.599 --> 51:24.420
earlier this week and, uh, with our parks manager Kyle. I told him

51:24.920 --> 51:28.700
that I think he should start coming to council meetings, especially when

51:29.200 --> 51:32.151
these things are on the Agenda. And then not only that,

51:32.504 --> 51:36.213
also direction to start sitting in with Steve. Steve's got

51:36.713 --> 51:40.372
a heavy lift right now, and we invited him to start sitting in

51:40.388 --> 51:44.257
on these— the monthly meetings, because I think that for

51:44.757 --> 51:47.838
now that most of what we're talking about affects him anyway, right?

51:47.934 --> 51:50.793
And Steve and I talked about that.

51:51.082 --> 51:54.598
And I did speak with Kyle, and he was not available to come tonight

51:55.098 --> 51:58.683
to this presentation. But he is planning on coming to our board meetings

51:59.199 --> 52:02.766
whenever they're available. Yes, sir. Definitely on the same page there. I think we

52:02.814 --> 52:06.353
saw that same hole, and I think that's important.

52:08.882 --> 52:12.566
Was there other comments on some of those,

52:12.680 --> 52:16.073
or anything you wanna see kind of pushed up the list?

52:16.639 --> 52:19.921
With that now out there, we already know

52:20.421 --> 52:21.547
there's some funding sources for some things.

52:24.580 --> 52:26.808
I would like to say, sorry, were you gonna hop in? No, go ahead.

52:27.305 --> 52:30.527
I would like to say that I really do support us having more

52:31.027 --> 52:34.823
regular rodeo ground maintenance. I know there's been kind of an evolution of what the

52:34.840 --> 52:38.398
city takes ownership of and what the nonprofits

52:38.898 --> 52:43.272
take ownership of and different things like that. But for it to be consistent

52:43.772 --> 52:47.087
and cared for with the proper equipment and all of those things,

52:48.546 --> 52:52.636
I would like for us to find out what that fiscal impact's

52:53.136 --> 52:55.731
gonna be on us to take that on, and whether we can work that into

52:55.779 --> 52:59.452
the schedule. And even if it's once a month to start instead

52:59.952 --> 53:03.445
of once a year, that at least is pushing us more in the right direction.

53:04.583 --> 53:08.448
Because it's a huge asset. If we support the Western

53:08.948 --> 53:12.458
way of life out here and our Western roots, then I think we should take

53:12.958 --> 53:16.373
care of one of the, you know, The biggest amenities that we have that

53:16.873 --> 53:20.289
reflects that. So I really support that. I'm glad that it's on the list.

53:21.605 --> 53:25.810
Yeah, I just thought, well done. I'm grateful

53:26.310 --> 53:29.694
for the presentation, Jesse. I would just say that I'm supportive of looking at

53:30.194 --> 53:33.931
all of what you've brought forward. I think the ADA ramps is

53:34.060 --> 53:37.783
a pretty straightforward thing, assuming that our staff finds

53:37.880 --> 53:41.232
that they'll be compliant. They're in the right location,

53:42.626 --> 53:45.943
and I like that there's already a ready-made funding source to take care of that

53:46.007 --> 53:48.684
that comes from outside of the city.

53:49.036 --> 53:52.417
Okay, those those CBDG funds I

53:52.562 --> 53:56.872
think can be used for that. The trail signage certainly okay

53:57.372 --> 53:59.741
with that. We it's just a matter of where do we find the resources to

53:59.933 --> 54:03.363
tackle that. The Smith Ranch benches I love what

54:03.863 --> 54:07.448
you said, Councilmember Clark. I think. I think offering those as a memorial

54:07.464 --> 54:10.997
bench and having folks purchase or donate

54:11.029 --> 54:14.449
them makes a lot of sense to me. And if not,

54:14.578 --> 54:18.004
then we can look at something else. Rodeo maintenance, I don't disagree with

54:18.036 --> 54:21.379
that. I guess the question that I have and the suggestion I would make is

54:22.070 --> 54:27.201
if we're going to put more resources into maintaining that, then should

54:27.701 --> 54:30.725
we be looking for an opportunity to rent that space out?

54:31.516 --> 54:35.545
Reserve that space to do something that's outside of perhaps,

54:35.561 --> 54:38.591
and maybe that's a good or maybe that's a bad idea, but I just want

54:39.091 --> 54:41.041
to open up that at least that line of thought.

54:42.887 --> 54:45.974
The wildlife— can I jump in on that for just a second? Just so I

54:46.474 --> 54:50.137
remember this year, it would have been our budget discussion

54:50.637 --> 54:53.768
a year ago. We bought a new tractor and I said keep that old tractor

54:54.268 --> 54:56.679
and let's use it over there. I don't know how I'd have to get some

54:57.179 --> 54:59.493
feedback from staff about how much the old ones used compared to the new one,

54:59.509 --> 55:02.203
but we only ever had one. Now we have two. And the thought was that

55:02.703 --> 55:06.374
it could sit over there, just like mowing the grass at the soccer field.

55:06.439 --> 55:09.802
We don't necessarily rent that out. It would get too much abuse

55:10.302 --> 55:13.758
possibly without the, you know, things in this type of

55:14.258 --> 55:17.771
an industry get damaged. But I think open

55:18.271 --> 55:21.788
riding for our residents and having mowed grass type of— I'm open

55:21.821 --> 55:25.306
to all that, Mayor, and I'm not disagreeing with you in any way.

55:25.435 --> 55:28.700
You're the horseman. And I'll certainly respect that.

55:28.780 --> 55:32.200
For me, it's just a matter of can we find it, can we

55:32.700 --> 55:36.060
find a way to make that more useful? And I think that's probably the goal.

55:36.560 --> 55:38.980
Yeah, and I wanna just, so for context, I guess what I'm saying is just

55:39.480 --> 55:41.260
like putting gas in a lawnmower and mowing the grass once a week, we're gonna

55:41.760 --> 55:44.840
put gas in a tractor that we already own and take 15 minutes to plow

55:45.340 --> 55:48.500
the dirt. That's the extent. And if that's all it takes, then let's, I mean—

55:49.000 --> 55:52.300
I think that's what they're asking. Yeah. Can I, let me finish. Go ahead.

55:52.800 --> 55:56.315
Maybe one step further on that. Uh, I think that—

55:56.815 --> 56:00.005
aren't we also supposed to be using the tractor over in the Memorial Park as

56:00.021 --> 56:02.428
well for, uh, the cemetery?

56:04.930 --> 56:06.598
Isn't that why we kept the old one? I think is the question.

56:08.656 --> 56:10.440
And I don't know how much use is over there or not.

56:13.150 --> 56:15.843
I, I don't know off the top of my head. I can't, I can't answer

56:16.343 --> 56:19.990
and say that it's this many hours, but yeah, there's several different pieces that equipment

56:20.490 --> 56:23.690
that gets transported around and used for several different items. If we

56:24.190 --> 56:26.550
don't have— Both tractors are in use, I guess, would be— Yeah, we use both

56:27.050 --> 56:30.070
of them. Just at this point, our city's getting big enough we could have a

56:30.570 --> 56:33.850
third one, right, Brad? Well, it's just time and money,

56:34.350 --> 56:37.950
right? I mean, I've, I've taken time and I've, I've groomed the rodeo grounds.

56:38.450 --> 56:41.610
I, I— you say 15 minutes, but I think it's longer than 15 minutes to

56:42.110 --> 56:45.350
turn that arena. I think it's a couple-hour deal. And if you're going to

56:45.850 --> 56:49.590
do it 3 times a week, those, those numbers add up. And so I think

56:50.090 --> 56:53.100
we just got to look within our budgets and see what we can facilitate.

56:53.181 --> 56:56.547
And if it's a service level thing and we want to bolster it

56:57.047 --> 57:00.876
up and we want to provide the services and more time and money and staff,

57:00.892 --> 57:04.483
then, then we can, we can do these things for sure. Yeah, go ahead and

57:04.983 --> 57:08.074
finish, Brett. All right. And then probably just the last one that I'll comment on,

57:08.587 --> 57:12.300
and everything else that's been said I agree with, but the wildlife Utility boxes.

57:12.800 --> 57:16.060
I like that idea. And I, again, there's, I think,

57:16.560 --> 57:19.720
a ready-made funding source that's available for that. And I think that you

57:20.220 --> 57:23.480
tried to identify that. So you, I thought I was waiting for you to weigh

57:23.980 --> 57:27.880
in on this. 0.25%. Yeah, the quarter. The quarter percent that we're collecting for

57:28.380 --> 57:31.200
capital projects. On capital projects. On capital projects that's being set aside into a special

57:31.700 --> 57:35.340
fund for art. I think it would be nice to start to put some

57:35.840 --> 57:39.790
of those funds to work. And I personally think this is a

57:40.290 --> 57:43.570
good place to start. Are there other things we should

57:44.070 --> 57:48.250
consider? Certainly, but this would start to give some return on those

57:48.750 --> 57:52.410
collections that we've been taking. Just for context, I think there's— we

57:52.910 --> 57:55.210
were trying to figure that out today. We got a phone call and Ben and

57:55.710 --> 57:59.810
I were at the Capitol. There's about $49,000? $48,000, I believe.

58:00.310 --> 58:03.750
$48,000? At one time. $47,000 in that account

58:04.250 --> 58:07.472
just on capital Probably just a quarter of 1%. Yeah, and I don't think we

58:07.972 --> 58:10.177
need to spend it all here, but I think that you could certainly identify,

58:10.306 --> 58:14.919
you know, a half dozen or 10 of the most impactful

58:14.999 --> 58:18.654
locations and then work through, 'cause we have to develop

58:19.154 --> 58:22.324
the process as to how you're gonna pick artists and who's gonna decide what

58:22.438 --> 58:26.084
goes on there. And that's a whole nother can of worms

58:26.584 --> 58:30.008
that we gotta work through. All right, I wanna

58:30.508 --> 58:34.398
go back to Councilmember Wood real quick. Was there more on that one? I just

58:34.898 --> 58:38.029
want to jump back to the rodeo grounds. I think that we need to make

58:38.529 --> 58:41.431
a decision of whether or not it's a park or not. If we're going to

58:41.480 --> 58:44.657
treat it like it's a— it's always going to be in disrepair.

58:45.868 --> 58:48.967
If we're going to treat it like it's a park, then we need to make

58:49.467 --> 58:52.326
it available to people. People need to be aware that it's there for their use

58:52.521 --> 58:56.131
and we need to maintain it as such. But I think we

58:56.195 --> 58:59.536
can't continue to pretend like it's not something that we need

59:00.036 --> 59:02.313
to maintain. Because we're really touching it once a year.

59:04.740 --> 59:07.439
So if we're going to make it available to everybody, then what is the use

59:07.939 --> 59:10.268
that we're going to make it available for? Are we allowing it to be a

59:10.768 --> 59:14.649
riding arena? If so, that tells you exactly what type of surface we

59:15.149 --> 59:17.177
need to put in there as far as the dirt goes. If we're going to

59:17.226 --> 59:20.158
let people come in and do other types of things that's going to commingle the

59:20.658 --> 59:23.010
dirt, then we'll have to scrape it before the rodeo every year.

59:26.587 --> 59:30.402
Um, I had a similar thought to Council Member

59:31.011 --> 59:34.601
Wright about, well, maybe we should be charging for it and different

59:35.101 --> 59:38.400
things. But then as I was sitting here thinking about it, we don't really charge

59:38.900 --> 59:42.936
anyone to come and use our pickleball courts or our tennis courts or anything like

59:43.436 --> 59:46.960
that. It's an amenity that we put in. Um, if someone's

59:47.460 --> 59:51.063
going to— Maybe just schedule it. Yeah, schedule it. Or if someone's

59:51.563 --> 59:54.340
going to make money from it, like if they're going to host a A big

59:54.566 --> 59:57.586
event that's— 4-H. Yeah, 4-H something or other,

59:57.651 --> 59:59.798
or something that's not necessarily city-driven.

01:00:04.108 --> 01:00:07.528
I, I think maybe then we could look at some sort of

01:00:07.802 --> 01:00:10.561
fee schedule or something. But in general,

01:00:11.254 --> 01:00:14.979
I think that it's our duty as a council to figure

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:18.457
out how to fund the maintenance and care of that

01:00:18.587 --> 01:00:20.836
space. And then, um,

01:00:21.976 --> 01:00:26.295
as we go through people using it, if there's inappropriate uses

01:00:26.407 --> 01:00:29.538
or something like that, let's cross that bridge when we

01:00:29.619 --> 01:00:33.055
come to it. Yeah, I just— my understanding is that it is in

01:00:33.555 --> 01:00:35.881
use already. It is open to the public year-round.

01:00:37.005 --> 01:00:41.436
People come in, they bring their dogs, use it as a dog park during

01:00:41.936 --> 01:00:45.909
the off-season, and people go in and Do other sort of recreational

01:00:46.409 --> 01:00:49.709
activities there. It's just a question of, you know, we have

01:00:50.209 --> 01:00:54.172
an agreement that puts the burden of maintenance on a particular group

01:00:54.672 --> 01:00:58.655
and it's not working out and how should we move forward?

01:01:03.419 --> 01:01:06.896
I think that's the suggestion that they're making is that perhaps the city should look

01:01:07.396 --> 01:01:10.303
at that and say, is there a different approach we can take to for the

01:01:10.803 --> 01:01:14.555
maintenance of it so that it becomes an even more utilized

01:01:14.571 --> 01:01:18.436
facility than what it is currently? I mean, it's certainly being

01:01:18.452 --> 01:01:20.475
utilized, and I'm aware of that, Marcus.

01:01:22.760 --> 01:01:26.131
But I think that at least what I'm hearing is how could we make it

01:01:26.228 --> 01:01:29.693
a little more useful for more people? That's what I'm hearing.

01:01:30.370 --> 01:01:34.138
Yeah, I would agree with that. I mean, I think that we've listed

01:01:34.638 --> 01:01:37.500
it as one of our parks. That's supposed to be what the use of it

01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:41.420
is, so we should be maintaining it as such. And to Melissa's point, I don't

01:01:41.920 --> 01:01:44.760
think that we should be charging people unless they're trying to reserve it like a

01:01:45.260 --> 01:01:48.020
pavilion. If they want the full use of the facility, then they go through the

01:01:48.520 --> 01:01:52.560
same planning or the event— what is that—

01:01:53.060 --> 01:01:56.860
reservation that everybody else does for pavilions or a portion of the park. And we

01:01:57.360 --> 01:02:00.360
have a fee schedule that's set up for that. Otherwise, it's available for people to

01:02:00.860 --> 01:02:04.689
use, and there probably ought to be some At least some restrictions about what

01:02:04.722 --> 01:02:06.021
could be done on the dirt.

01:02:07.794 --> 01:02:10.044
So I think what I've seen in other cities, I'm just floating this out there

01:02:10.544 --> 01:02:14.027
for you guys to consider. For instance, it's just open riding, and the

01:02:14.527 --> 01:02:16.585
key here is that the ground's in a condition that it can be rode,

01:02:16.844 --> 01:02:20.033
you know, rode in. Compare it to a pavilion. The pavilion's there,

01:02:20.226 --> 01:02:22.561
there's tables there, you can sit down, you can eat lunch, you can come and

01:02:23.061 --> 01:02:26.540
go as you please. It's open to everybody. If you want to rent the pavilion

01:02:27.040 --> 01:02:29.679
or block it off to where others can't use it because you have a birthday

01:02:30.179 --> 01:02:33.521
party, You call the city and you reserve the pavilion for $30 an

01:02:34.021 --> 01:02:36.297
hour or whatever that is, and then you have your event and then you go

01:02:36.313 --> 01:02:39.955
on. And that's, that's exactly how Spanish Fork

01:02:40.455 --> 01:02:43.132
and other arenas do that. That's what I'm talking about. So, so there's— if you're

01:02:43.212 --> 01:02:46.277
the 4-H and you want to block off from 7 to 9 on a Monday

01:02:46.293 --> 01:02:49.678
night, you call in and you pay your $30 an hour

01:02:50.031 --> 01:02:53.417
fee type of deal, and— or $50 or whatever, that would be

01:02:53.917 --> 01:02:57.556
in the fee schedule. But it's open riding unless you're bringing in an

01:02:57.588 --> 01:03:00.879
approved use. I agree with that, but I also think

01:03:01.379 --> 01:03:05.840
that we should have an application for a fee waiver for specific

01:03:05.857 --> 01:03:09.630
things that are serving our community. For example, the FFA

01:03:10.130 --> 01:03:14.029
kids at our local high school may need it for something specific,

01:03:14.286 --> 01:03:17.963
and they may need it to be blocked off specifically for them, but they're

01:03:17.995 --> 01:03:21.993
not a for-profit entity. Mutton busting. Yeah, mutton busting.

01:03:22.154 --> 01:03:25.669
They are kids that are growing up here in this community And I think

01:03:26.169 --> 01:03:29.792
that there should be a mechanism by which organizations

01:03:30.292 --> 01:03:33.610
and people can request that that fee is not assessed

01:03:34.110 --> 01:03:37.861
or waived. So that's just something that I also think that

01:03:38.361 --> 01:03:41.807
the Community Services Board could help tackle. Okay. I was just gonna

01:03:42.307 --> 01:03:45.384
say, 'cause I haven't chimed in, I'm very supportive of what the Community Services

01:03:45.416 --> 01:03:49.330
Board's been doing. Super grateful for what your work is, and I'm really good

01:03:49.830 --> 01:03:53.358
