01:15:24.574 --> 01:15:28.115
that there has to be transportation relief to our area.

01:15:28.660 --> 01:15:32.121
And some of those transportation funding dollars are already programmed out to

01:15:32.621 --> 01:15:36.816
2032 and beyond. And so some of the ideas were, how can

01:15:36.912 --> 01:15:40.293
we get state funding allocated to

01:15:40.358 --> 01:15:43.899
meet the needs of our area and

01:15:44.399 --> 01:15:47.925
do so in a manner that we could either self or work with our

01:15:47.990 --> 01:15:51.442
development partners to try to self-fund some of those projects

01:15:51.942 --> 01:15:55.360
with state dollars coming in in later years to help program and offset that.

01:15:56.002 --> 01:15:59.631
That would benefit us specifically because we would be

01:16:00.131 --> 01:16:03.821
able to undertake those projects today and then provide our residents immediate

01:16:03.870 --> 01:16:07.274
relief. But second and most important, we would be able to

01:16:07.338 --> 01:16:10.726
do it at a less expensive price than it would be to construct

01:16:11.226 --> 01:16:14.387
in 2030. 2033, 2034. And so if we can do those things up

01:16:14.887 --> 01:16:18.387
front, that saves dollars for the state as well over their long term, and it

01:16:18.419 --> 01:16:22.177
opens up things for, for our entire region. The concerns

01:16:22.193 --> 01:16:25.791
expressed by House Speaker Schultz was that he wants to make sure that we

01:16:26.291 --> 01:16:29.421
don't circumvent or break the process. We have processes in the state of Utah

01:16:29.501 --> 01:16:33.404
for funding, but in articulating

01:16:34.560 --> 01:16:38.975
that the processes that we have had locally have been maybe

01:16:39.475 --> 01:16:42.825
a little bit broken to some degree because we have larger geographic

01:16:42.906 --> 01:16:46.291
areas with less representation on boards like MAG,

01:16:46.997 --> 01:16:50.654
where other parts of the county have smaller municipalities

01:16:50.686 --> 01:16:53.702
with lots of mayors and funding can go to different areas.

01:16:54.922 --> 01:16:58.371
And everybody wants a bite at the apple, and yet we really need to look

01:16:58.871 --> 01:17:02.622
at what are the, what are the roads that are failing and

01:17:03.122 --> 01:17:05.054
how do we address those failing roads, and how do we do it in the

01:17:05.554 --> 01:17:09.629
most expeditious way possible. And so our

01:17:09.726 --> 01:17:13.008
ask was really not to circumvent or earmark or circumvent a

01:17:13.056 --> 01:17:16.115
process. It was really how do we fix the process so we can work together

01:17:16.615 --> 01:17:20.618
to meet the needs and demands of our area. The mayor did

01:17:21.118 --> 01:17:24.993
an exceptionally good job sharing our story that we're

01:17:25.156 --> 01:17:29.455
adding as a community more than 200 cars per month, adding to

01:17:29.955 --> 01:17:33.016
our overall region when you add Saratoga and West Lehigh to it,

01:17:33.530 --> 01:17:37.159
nearly 500 cars per month. And the programming dollars are already spent.

01:17:37.239 --> 01:17:40.483
And by the time we hit the next available funding cycle,

01:17:40.996 --> 01:17:44.513
we will have 24,000 more cars on our streets.

01:17:45.027 --> 01:17:47.836
And sharing that story and laying it out on a map,

01:17:48.158 --> 01:17:51.481
but doing so in an empathetic way, saying, look, we, we have a desire to

01:17:51.594 --> 01:17:54.789
