vehicle question and the police vehicles and on-call vehicles and whatnot. Now,

02:40:18.795 --> 02:40:22.449
every city employee has a vehicle that they take home. It is those

02:40:22.481 --> 02:40:25.773
employees who are on call or would have responsibilities to

02:40:26.273 --> 02:40:29.999
come out during an emergency or during a fire, during like as far as

02:40:30.031 --> 02:40:31.879
building inspection, a water break.

02:40:33.165 --> 02:40:36.218
And we have those at all hours of the day and night. And when we

02:40:36.718 --> 02:40:39.849
have an emergency need, our person— Every hour of the day.

02:40:39.897 --> 02:40:43.095
Yeah, exactly. Every time there's an emergency, we have

02:40:43.111 --> 02:40:46.245
to have personnel that can respond with the tools that they need to be able

02:40:46.325 --> 02:40:50.712
to immediately provide benefit to the residents

02:40:51.212 --> 02:40:51.447
incidents and not drive all the way back down to the Public Works building,

02:40:51.479 --> 02:40:55.169
which is on the south end of our city. If that problem— let's say

02:40:55.669 --> 02:40:58.057
they live in the ranches or they even live in Saratoga, they'd have to drive

02:40:58.201 --> 02:41:01.747
all the way to past City Center near the data centers

02:41:02.247 --> 02:41:05.533
where our Public Works facility is and drive all the way back in order to

02:41:06.033 --> 02:41:09.191
respond. And homes could be damaged, water could be

02:41:09.691 --> 02:41:12.930
lost, all sorts of problems could take place if our personnel

02:41:13.042 --> 02:41:16.429
didn't have the on-call vehicles with the tools they need to do the job effectively

02:41:16.929 --> 02:41:20.413
for the safety of our residents. Specifically, it was talked about with

02:41:20.445 --> 02:41:24.541
public safety and police officers. Of course, the Sheriff's Department

02:41:25.041 --> 02:41:27.432
is separate and they have their own policies when it comes to that. But having

02:41:27.932 --> 02:41:31.336
been a city manager in both Utah County, outside of Utah County,

02:41:31.512 --> 02:41:35.367
and in the entire nation, I can tell you that on-call

02:41:35.867 --> 02:41:39.560
vehicles or police officers across the nation, typically the standard

02:41:40.060 --> 02:41:43.904
is that they do take their vehicles home, and that is especially held

02:41:44.404 --> 02:41:46.850
true in the state of Utah. I'm from New York. I lived— I've worked in

02:41:47.027 --> 02:41:51.604
Iowa. I've worked in other places. The reason that

02:41:51.701 --> 02:41:55.758
police officers, especially in the state of Utah, take their vehicles home is because

02:41:56.258 --> 02:42:00.001
the prevailing feeling is that police vehicles on the road

02:42:00.501 --> 02:42:04.203
create a sense of safety and stability, even if they're in the commute mode.

02:42:04.950 --> 02:42:08.888
And having those vehicles out out and about instills safety so that people drive

02:42:09.065 --> 02:42:12.547
better, they're more cognizant of what's going on, and there's more security.

02:42:12.612 --> 02:42:15.789
And if an officer actually did need to respond and they were off duty,

02:42:16.207 --> 02:42:18.646
they would have the tools and equipment that they need to be able to respond

02:42:19.146 --> 02:42:23.236
as well. This is so much so in the United States

02:42:23.285 --> 02:42:27.136
that the difference between regular take-home vehicles and police

02:42:27.636 --> 02:42:31.438
department take-home vehicles is that the IRS requires

02:42:31.550 --> 02:42:34.970
that if a take-home vehicle goes home, let's say, with somebody who's on

02:42:35.146 --> 02:42:38.647
call, that commute time is actually a taxable

02:42:40.059 --> 02:42:43.576
benefit to that employee. And that taxable benefit goes on

02:42:44.076 --> 02:42:47.702
their paycheck and whatnot, and they're responsible for that— those miles and the

02:42:48.151 --> 02:42:52.470
fuel that they would use in their commute time. But police vehicles

02:42:52.502 --> 02:42:56.019
are exempt from that by IRS standards, and that is because of

02:42:56.519 --> 02:42:59.840
the overall greater good of having police vehicles on the street. And so police

02:42:59.968 --> 02:43:03.501
officers, sheriff's department deputies, they are not taxed

02:43:04.001 --> 02:43:07.017
for their commute because they could be called upon to respond at any time for

02:43:07.114 --> 02:43:11.288
any emergency anywhere in the state. And they're all licensed by

02:43:11.788 --> 02:43:15.399
post throughout the entire state. And so it's not just a jurisdictional

02:43:15.899 --> 02:43:18.851
thing. They could respond anywhere. So I just wanted to answer some of those questions.

02:43:18.899 --> 02:43:21.854
Hopefully I've hit them all. There's a couple more I can touch on,

02:43:21.870 --> 02:43:25.592
but also just On that vehicle one, why are

02:43:26.092 --> 02:43:28.591
they not here? Because they're parked at our Public Works. We have limited stalls.

02:43:28.688 --> 02:43:31.093
We barely have enough spaces. But we do have several that are parked here.

02:43:31.350 --> 02:43:34.621
And we do have some that are here. And then also on the sheriff

02:43:35.121 --> 02:43:38.486
ones, even though it's a need, newsflash, we pay

02:43:38.986 --> 02:43:42.496
$9.8 million no matter where they park because we're on contract. So it doesn't

02:43:42.996 --> 02:43:46.906
save us or cost us. It's absolutely a set price. And I don't care

02:43:47.406 --> 02:43:50.306
where you drive that car because it doesn't cost me any money. But these were

02:43:50.354 --> 02:43:52.824
all good questions, and we're glad that you asked them so we could answer them

02:43:53.324 --> 02:43:56.240
for the entire public. Yeah, yeah, so that's— Mayor?

02:43:56.993 --> 02:44:00.923
Yeah. I think there was one more, and I think it stems from what you

02:44:01.423 --> 02:44:05.846
use the money for. There was kind of this parallel idea

02:44:05.974 --> 02:44:09.663
of like maybe a family budget and going grocery shopping and

02:44:10.163 --> 02:44:13.448
getting the extras or the fluff. And I think a lot of people see the

02:44:13.948 --> 02:44:17.220
parks as The difference between a

02:44:17.720 --> 02:44:21.124
family budget and the city budget is there

02:44:21.624 --> 02:44:24.899
are certain funds that can only be used for certain things.

02:44:25.557 --> 02:44:29.654
And when there's parks funds that come from parks impact fees, we can't

02:44:30.154 --> 02:44:33.718
just shift those over and use them for water or sewer or

02:44:33.830 --> 02:44:37.766
for an additional employee or anything like that. They have to very specifically

02:44:38.266 --> 02:44:41.702
be used according to state law for the things that

02:44:42.202 --> 02:44:45.490
they were collected for. for. So while, um,

02:44:45.795 --> 02:44:48.460
I had the same question before I ran for office, I was like, why are

02:44:48.960 --> 02:44:53.194
we spending money on Corey Ride Park? Um, and before

02:44:53.259 --> 02:44:56.693
it was built. And I, I think that it's helpful to understand

02:44:57.127 --> 02:45:00.866
the legal limitations of certain dollars within

02:45:00.963 --> 02:45:04.590
a municipal budget. I, I think that is a really good point. And I maybe

02:45:05.090 --> 02:45:08.906
just to add on to that as well, um, we do have certain assets in

02:45:09.406 --> 02:45:12.432
our community, and we want to make, make sure that we take care of and

02:45:12.464 --> 02:45:16.198
maintain those. It's our responsibility. Those are your assets, and we want

02:45:16.698 --> 02:45:19.916
to make sure we preserve them. And, um, to not take care of some of

02:45:20.416 --> 02:45:23.361
those assets would be equivalent to you not changing the oil in your car.

02:45:24.130 --> 02:45:27.111
Um, it might get you by, and you might not be able to— you might

02:45:27.611 --> 02:45:29.242
be able to live for a period of time, but eventually you're going to have

02:45:29.274 --> 02:45:32.527
to replace that engine. And so we want to make sure that we maintain

02:45:32.656 --> 02:45:36.298
our assets to the very best of our ability for our residents. And yet our

02:45:36.394 --> 02:45:39.863
level of service is really strained because we're spread very, very thin. And that's why

02:45:40.363 --> 02:45:44.053
the council's looking to bring more staff on to be able to help with

02:45:44.102 --> 02:45:47.682
enhancing our parks and open spaces. Can you also address

02:45:47.811 --> 02:45:51.471
how it costs $250,000 to engineer something?

02:45:51.664 --> 02:45:54.779
That's the last thing that I was waiting to get on right here. Yeah,

02:45:54.795 --> 02:45:58.569
and do you— and what's the process of determining you're going to choose that particular

02:45:59.069 --> 02:46:02.260
engineer? So we do have a procurement process, first of all, and this is something

02:46:02.760 --> 02:46:06.130
that I did for a living. Not the procurement, but the actual construction.

02:46:06.540 --> 02:46:09.800
And when we say design a road, you know, for $200,000,

02:46:10.300 --> 02:46:13.900
first of all, the $250,000, we had talked about this earlier today and we

02:46:14.400 --> 02:46:17.920
think it's going to be 70% of that. But this is a budget amount.

02:46:18.420 --> 02:46:20.400
This is what we're putting in the budget so that we can make sure that

02:46:20.900 --> 02:46:23.100
we can get the job done. Doesn't mean that's what it's going to cost.

02:46:23.600 --> 02:46:25.958
Doesn't mean that's the bid amount. That's how much we have in our budget to

02:46:25.974 --> 02:46:30.116
make sure that we get the job done. We think that that design's probably

02:46:30.181 --> 02:46:33.697
closer to $200,000. But what that covers is engineering

02:46:34.355 --> 02:46:38.289
for road base and inspections and the water

02:46:38.305 --> 02:46:41.966
line and the sewer line. It's not just the surface that you see. It's everything

02:46:42.062 --> 02:46:45.804
underneath it and designing everything

02:46:45.852 --> 02:46:48.341
from the roads, the curves. I'm sure you want to weigh in, so I'm just

02:46:48.841 --> 02:46:50.845
going to let you go ahead. Sure. And so there's other things too. There's also

02:46:51.345 --> 02:46:55.320
signal I mean, you know, conduits, controls, being that they're— it's more complicated than

02:46:55.820 --> 02:46:58.720
that, especially when you widen a road. You also have to get, you know,

02:46:59.220 --> 02:47:02.360
on-site survey as well as look at the elevations, because roads aren't actually flat,

02:47:02.860 --> 02:47:05.420
they're actually curved. If they were flat, we would have water standing on there and

02:47:05.920 --> 02:47:08.180
you wouldn't be able to drive on it. So we have to look at what's

02:47:08.680 --> 02:47:11.700
the curvature of the existing road, make sure we match it so it drains properly,

02:47:12.000 --> 02:47:15.040
we don't have standing water out there. So then that drain is going to

02:47:15.540 --> 02:47:17.782
go into the storm drain pipe that's actually underground. Yeah,

02:47:18.824 --> 02:47:22.272
where does that water go once we design that? It can't have a belly in

02:47:22.772 --> 02:47:24.261
it or it's no longer going to— it has to go downhill and then it's

02:47:24.761 --> 02:47:27.661
got to be pumped up and it's got to go downhill, right? That's all design.

02:47:27.709 --> 02:47:31.333
The size of the pump, how much water— if it's 7 lanes

02:47:31.833 --> 02:47:34.909
wide as opposed to 2 lanes wide, how much surface is that in

02:47:34.941 --> 02:47:38.549
a 100-year storm? Can that pipe hold it? That's a lot of math. How many

02:47:39.049 --> 02:47:41.548
hours is that? It's a lot.

02:47:42.302 --> 02:47:44.098
It's a lot of hours. So a lot of engineers, there's site surveys, verification.

02:47:44.598 --> 02:47:46.478
I would just like to add to the point of this is that not only

02:47:46.511 --> 02:47:50.541
do we comply with state procurement laws, we comply with

02:47:51.041 --> 02:47:54.521
our own city procurement laws and responsibilities, our codes, to make

02:47:55.021 --> 02:47:58.481
sure that we advertise properly, that we receive

02:47:58.562 --> 02:48:01.359
bids from all competent contractors,

02:48:04.050 --> 02:48:07.461
that we go through evaluation process, a scoring process. It's very

02:48:07.477 --> 02:48:10.862
regimented. We have a purchasing person. We're going to be bringing

02:48:10.958 --> 02:48:14.309
on another because we have so many projects, but it just doesn't include that one

02:48:14.405 --> 02:48:17.788
purchasing person. Depending upon the project, if it's streets or

02:48:18.288 --> 02:48:21.587
if it's parks or water or whatever, we have a whole group that score

02:48:21.699 --> 02:48:25.050
those things out. And it's not necessarily always the lowest bid,

02:48:25.082 --> 02:48:28.224
I will admit that. It is the greatest value

02:48:28.288 --> 02:48:31.655
to the city, and we have to look at that based on their

02:48:32.155 --> 02:48:35.488
qualifications, what work they've performed in the past. You know, if someone right out of

02:48:35.988 --> 02:48:39.650
college versus somebody who's done a sewer treatment project

02:48:40.167 --> 02:48:44.038
of 6 million gallons per day before, and that they have proven

02:48:44.135 --> 02:48:46.895
competence to be able to do that. We want to make sure that we have

02:48:47.395 --> 02:48:50.070
the right people on the job and that we're using the taxpayers' resources to the

02:48:50.570 --> 02:48:55.261
very best of our ability. We consider those dollars to be exceptionally

02:48:57.069 --> 02:49:00.523
important and, and, and, and treat those with the greatest of

02:49:01.023 --> 02:49:04.190
respect. And typically that does land on the lowest bid a lot of times.

02:49:04.290 --> 02:49:08.130
A lot of times they are. A lot of times, sometimes they're qualification-based

02:49:08.630 --> 02:49:12.230
as well. Yep. So, all right, I think we've answered most of those questions.

02:49:12.250 --> 02:49:15.790
And again, we're happy to speak to anyone. You know, one thing

02:49:16.290 --> 02:49:18.150
we're trying to do is make sure that the information is out there because the

02:49:18.650 --> 02:49:21.610
last thing we want is to leave that unanswered. So if we didn't answer something,

02:49:22.110 --> 02:49:25.610
by all means, we've all posted our phone numbers and we love

02:49:26.110 --> 02:49:29.148
talking to residents to help you understand why we're where we're at. There's one more

02:49:29.648 --> 02:49:32.775
question that didn't get answered. So it was brought up that the presentation that Terrence

02:49:33.275 --> 02:49:36.850
did did not itemize the expenses, but if

02:49:37.350 --> 02:49:40.990
you, if you go to the public agenda, it itemizes every single

02:49:41.424 --> 02:49:44.890
item. So if you go to eaglemountain.gov, you click

02:49:45.390 --> 02:49:48.693
on public meetings, you go click on the public meetings portal,

02:49:49.928 --> 02:49:53.635
and then you click on today's meeting, which it'll scan right

02:49:54.135 --> 02:49:55.801
down to it. You click on today's meeting, you click on the agenda item,

02:49:56.363 --> 02:50:00.185
And you'll see a budget worksheet that itemizes everything line

02:50:00.217 --> 02:50:02.882
by line by line. And that's also in your packet, so you've already studied it,

02:50:02.898 --> 02:50:06.238
so this was more of a highlight, correct? Right, and the presentation that Terrence

02:50:06.738 --> 02:50:10.702
did was more of a, it was not intended to go line

02:50:10.734 --> 02:50:14.154
by line by line by line on everything. He was sort of hitting some highlights

02:50:14.588 --> 02:50:17.847
that gave us an opportunity to ask questions, but we've been given this

02:50:18.040 --> 02:50:20.689
line by line by line by line in advance so we could read through it.

02:50:21.380 --> 02:50:24.462
So we could give Terrence questions, but it's there for

02:50:24.962 --> 02:50:26.999
anybody to see. It's there for the public to see. It was not hidden.

02:50:27.979 --> 02:50:31.736
And then the other thing I will say, so when it comes to a city

02:50:31.768 --> 02:50:34.032
hall, because this has been brought up a lot,

02:50:35.204 --> 02:50:38.480
it's a long process to build a city hall.

02:50:39.299 --> 02:50:42.205
And you have to, you've got to buy the land and you got to design

02:50:42.705 --> 02:50:46.604
and it takes, you can't just do it all at the same time. So you

02:50:47.104 --> 02:50:50.631
just, Budget for it slowly, year by year by year, by completing step after

02:50:51.131 --> 02:50:53.631
step after step. So are we progressing down steps? Yes,

02:50:54.320 --> 02:50:57.480
because we need to. And if you look at other cities our size, they have

02:50:57.529 --> 02:51:00.865
city halls, and we're living in rented trailers.

02:51:02.084 --> 02:51:05.581
And and I would invite you to talk to our staff and ask them.

02:51:05.629 --> 02:51:09.126
Do your own independent analysis. Ask the staff if we have enough space.

02:51:10.121 --> 02:51:13.730
Don't just trust what we have researched. Go ask them. I'm gonna say

02:51:14.230 --> 02:51:17.010
something on that real quick. Quick if you want, because this just came up today.

02:51:18.740 --> 02:51:21.100
Fly a drone, go take a picture of this building and look at the square

02:51:21.600 --> 02:51:24.269
footage of this building. From where we're sitting, everything on this side of us is

02:51:24.769 --> 02:51:28.369
the library. There's no staff in there. This is the council chamber we use for

02:51:28.869 --> 02:51:31.178
planning commission. We use it every single day for trainings, for meetings.

02:51:32.405 --> 02:51:35.828
We have the sheriff's department in this quarter of the building

02:51:36.681 --> 02:51:40.644
on this side. We have over 200 employees, but I'll call it 180

02:51:41.144 --> 02:51:44.328
employees roughly, and that's over 200 now, that are in

02:51:44.521 --> 02:51:48.840
that quarter of the building, which has about— and

02:51:48.937 --> 02:51:50.992
plus 3/4 office, which is 4 more employees.

02:51:52.469 --> 02:51:55.761
9 offices for 200 employees. That's it.

02:51:56.146 --> 02:52:00.208
Well, you've got that in addition to the Public Works building. Yeah, but I mean,

02:52:01.123 --> 02:52:04.511
here, and, and the rent office space that we rent on our north end of

02:52:05.011 --> 02:52:08.012
town, all rented, and you can own any of the vehicles Cars parked outside of

02:52:08.028 --> 02:52:10.838
there every night. All rented, and there's not enough space over there.

02:52:10.934 --> 02:52:14.145
Here in this building, we have 9 offices for all of

02:52:14.645 --> 02:52:18.095
our— that we could put in one building, be more efficient, and save a

02:52:18.595 --> 02:52:21.596
ton of money. When we have a meeting here, and this is— I think the

02:52:22.096 --> 02:52:24.839
residents don't understand this about the City Hall. It's not because I want— when the

02:52:25.339 --> 02:52:29.094
City Hall gets built, I'll never see it. I'm building this— it's like plant

02:52:29.594 --> 02:52:32.289
a tree for somebody, for shade for somebody else. I'll never enjoy the shade of

02:52:32.789 --> 02:52:36.072
that building. The problem is when we have a meeting here

02:52:36.120 --> 02:52:39.005
and Steve needs to come from that building and Brad needs to come from this

02:52:39.505 --> 02:52:43.091
building, and we spend hours of wasted time

02:52:43.267 --> 02:52:45.767
coming to a meeting where we could be in the same building and have our—

02:52:46.152 --> 02:52:49.837
get our work done and go to work and get more accomplished. We will save

02:52:50.337 --> 02:52:53.507
money building a city hall. I will advocate for a city hall

02:52:54.388 --> 02:52:57.593
all day long that I will never sit in because it is the right thing

02:52:57.673 --> 02:53:00.962
to do and we need it. Our city is way overgrown

02:53:01.462 --> 02:53:04.608
for this building. It's not even close. So, you know, I mean,

02:53:04.625 --> 02:53:07.817
I'll have that conversation all day long. A city hall is

02:53:07.850 --> 02:53:11.126
not a want, it is a need, 100%.

02:53:11.787 --> 02:53:13.998
And I will advocate for that every day,

02:53:15.095 --> 02:53:18.636
even if I never sit in it. So that's

02:53:18.749 --> 02:53:21.675
the city hall. I kind of cut you off, Craig. I want to make sure—

02:53:22.175 --> 02:53:24.440
No, it's all right. You took my thunder and ran with it, and you did

02:53:24.940 --> 02:53:27.671
a great job with it. Because I think the number's important. We got like 9

02:53:28.171 --> 02:53:31.921
buildings or 9 offices. Well, to your point, and you said it,

02:53:31.953 --> 02:53:36.122
like, we're doing something that I'm not gonna— this is gonna

02:53:36.622 --> 02:53:40.164
be completed after our terms are done. And this is a lot of decisions

02:53:40.196 --> 02:53:43.291
we're making, and that's why I made the public comment earlier today. We're making decisions

02:53:43.323 --> 02:53:47.460
today to benefit the future. This is not benefiting us specifically.

02:53:48.134 --> 02:53:51.245
And another thing that I wrote down, I was just gonna let it go,

02:53:51.261 --> 02:53:54.773
but 99% of this— I can't

02:53:55.273 --> 02:53:57.675
reach back there. Will you pat my back a little bit? Because you already did

02:53:58.175 --> 02:54:01.043
earlier today. None of this is funded with tax dollars.

02:54:01.508 --> 02:54:04.250
Most of this is money that we've gone out and got from MAG,

02:54:04.635 --> 02:54:08.628
from other partners, from federal appropriations,

02:54:08.724 --> 02:54:12.878
from the state. Today we were talking about $600 million,

02:54:13.327 --> 02:54:16.197
$700 million, $800 million today.

02:54:16.678 --> 02:54:20.119
$500 million on the one road and $300 million

02:54:20.313 --> 02:54:23.646
with the extension of Mountain View, that I went up there and

02:54:24.146 --> 02:54:27.579
spent the whole day advocating for, that I think we just moved up 15 years.

02:54:28.322 --> 02:54:32.085
Wait, I can't reach again. I'm just kidding. It's not

02:54:32.392 --> 02:54:35.790
taxpayer money. But I think this is, this is the point, Mayor.

02:54:35.936 --> 02:54:39.059
I mean, this is something that I think our residents completely miss.

02:54:40.987 --> 02:54:44.145
Property taxes last year raised $2.8 million in Eagle Mountain city,

02:54:44.260 --> 02:54:46.787
period, across the board. That's all taxpayers.

02:54:48.190 --> 02:54:51.836
$2.8 million. Every single thing we touch,

02:54:51.852 --> 02:54:55.627
every project we touch is well in advance of $2.8 million.

