about the Open and Public Meetings Act.

02:58:25.034 --> 02:58:28.429
And, and maybe as an opening to this too, uh, this is Open and Public

02:58:28.929 --> 02:58:32.325
Meetings Act for the public's sake. Uh, once a year we're required to provide

02:58:32.825 --> 02:58:36.047
that training to our elected leaders. And so, Mayor, I, I would highly encourage you

02:58:36.547 --> 02:58:39.754
to ask our 2 council members who are not here to watch this portion of

02:58:40.254 --> 02:58:43.648
the meeting because this is really beneficial. But not only them, but our planning commissioners

02:58:43.760 --> 02:58:47.510
and any other boards that's really— actually, uh, so I delivered

02:58:48.010 --> 02:58:51.212
this to planning commission last week, and I talked to the

02:58:51.532 --> 02:58:54.337
board between the meetings and told them they should listen to this. So,

02:58:54.481 --> 02:58:58.647
and I know Charlotte's still here, um, so we can pass this information

02:58:59.147 --> 02:59:02.253
on. I believe Jesse might be online. So, um,

02:59:02.493 --> 02:59:05.891
she is online. So I think I think, to your

02:59:05.956 --> 02:59:09.043
point, I agree, and I think we need to make sure that gets out.

02:59:09.189 --> 02:59:12.777
Steve is— maybe we lost Steve, but whoever sits on those boards,

02:59:13.666 --> 02:59:16.919
make sure that we get that out. There he is. I just couldn't see

02:59:17.419 --> 02:59:21.766
you. Okay, so this is a combination

02:59:22.266 --> 02:59:26.376
Open Meetings Act training and Municipal Ethics Act training. So we're

02:59:26.828 --> 02:59:30.699
gonna start with a little video just to highlight the Importance

02:59:31.199 --> 02:59:35.117
of understanding the rules. Okay, please thumb—

02:59:35.667 --> 02:59:39.775
oh, there you go. I tell you, I gotta plead ignorance on this thing because

02:59:39.872 --> 02:59:43.258
if anyone had said anything to me at all when I first started here that

02:59:43.357 --> 02:59:44.930
that sort of thing was frowned upon,

02:59:46.430 --> 02:59:50.170
you know, because I've worked in a lot of offices and I tell

02:59:50.670 --> 02:59:53.830
you, people do that all the time. So, uh,

02:59:55.516 --> 02:59:58.374
It's important to understand the rules. Ignorance is not an excuse.

02:59:58.503 --> 03:00:02.083
It's a well-known maxim in the law. As an attorney,

03:00:02.774 --> 03:00:07.783
I always advise clients, you know, you need to inform yourself, because ultimately

03:00:07.799 --> 03:00:10.497
you're responsible to abide by the Open Meetings Act in this case.

03:00:11.509 --> 03:00:14.752
So the purpose of the Open Meetings Act is the intent of the legislature that

03:00:14.864 --> 03:00:17.578
the state, its agencies, and its political subdivisions,

03:00:18.172 --> 03:00:22.354
including cities, take their actions openly and conduct their deliberations openly.

03:00:22.773 --> 03:00:26.225
So that's why we have open meetings. That's why we make

03:00:26.725 --> 03:00:30.630
these decisions in meetings like this, is so that it can be done in

03:00:31.130 --> 03:00:35.039
the face of the public. These laws are important so that

03:00:35.539 --> 03:00:39.612
citizens can see how the process works. Okay. So just

03:00:40.112 --> 03:00:43.228
as an opener, potential penalties for violations of the Open

03:00:43.728 --> 03:00:47.020
Meetings Act are somebody could institute a suit to void a final

03:00:47.520 --> 03:00:50.766
action on something that's done in violation of the Act, that there are criminal

03:00:50.814 --> 03:00:54.410
punishments involved, and ultimately a violation by an

03:00:54.910 --> 03:00:57.686
elected official could lead to expulsion from office.

03:00:58.457 --> 03:01:02.262
Okay, so the default rule is

03:01:02.358 --> 03:01:06.228
that a meeting is open to the public. Meetings can be closed only for very

03:01:06.728 --> 03:01:10.066
specific reasons outlined in the statute. I think

03:01:10.566 --> 03:01:14.038
you're very familiar with What the reasons we usually close

03:01:14.538 --> 03:01:17.930
a meeting for are related to pending or reasonably imminent litigation,

03:01:18.543 --> 03:01:20.207
land purchases or sales,

03:01:21.874 --> 03:01:25.723
or discussing the competency of an individual.

03:01:26.319 --> 03:01:29.926
So whether that's in relation to hiring or firing, or if it's in relation to

03:01:30.410 --> 03:01:34.078
contracting, or whatever the case may be. So it requires

03:01:34.578 --> 03:01:38.120
a 2/3 supermajority vote to close a meeting. So in this case,

03:01:38.185 --> 03:01:40.106
it would typically require require 4 votes.

03:01:42.170 --> 03:01:45.673
Okay. So the question then is, what is a meeting? It is actually

03:01:46.173 --> 03:01:48.998
a term that is defined in the Open Meetings Act. It is the convening of

03:01:49.498 --> 03:01:53.142
a public body with a quorum present. So the convening is done

03:01:53.174 --> 03:01:56.443
by the person with authority to convene a meeting, which in

03:01:56.540 --> 03:02:00.146
our case is either the mayor or 2 council members.

03:02:02.333 --> 03:02:06.584
So with a quorum present, which is 3 individuals on

03:02:07.084 --> 03:02:08.943
the council exclusive of the mayor,

03:02:10.195 --> 03:02:13.790
including a workshop or an executive session, whether in person or by means

03:02:13.806 --> 03:02:17.418
of electronic communications, for the purpose of discussing, receiving comments from

03:02:17.918 --> 03:02:21.960
the public about, or acting upon a matter over which the public body has

03:02:22.056 --> 03:02:25.603
jurisdiction or advisory power. So the simplified

03:02:26.103 --> 03:02:29.246
version of this is a— you have a quorum, so 3 individuals, and you are

03:02:29.278 --> 03:02:31.589
discussing public business. Yes, are you going to say something?

03:02:32.680 --> 03:02:36.058
Craig? Oh, it looked like you were gonna say something, okay. So this, a meeting

03:02:36.090 --> 03:02:39.905
can include phone calls, video conferencing, emails, text messages, et cetera.

03:02:40.002 --> 03:02:43.735
It doesn't have to be a formal setting. So the

03:02:44.235 --> 03:02:47.707
rule of thumb is if you are in a quorum, don't talk about

03:02:48.207 --> 03:02:50.269
city business, which I know can be hard to do because what one thing do

03:02:50.285 --> 03:02:52.830
you all have in common? So,

03:02:54.196 --> 03:02:57.922
it's that you're all elected to represent the city. So as

03:02:58.018 --> 03:03:01.358
I said, a quorum is 3 members of the city council exclusive of the mayor.

03:03:01.422 --> 03:03:04.682
It's specific in the statute. The mayor does not count

03:03:04.698 --> 03:03:08.359
in determining whether a quorum exists. Chances of social meetings

03:03:08.535 --> 03:03:11.201
are not subject to the Open Public Meetings Act,

03:03:12.245 --> 03:03:15.761
but they cannot be used to circumvent the requirements of the act. So if you

03:03:16.261 --> 03:03:18.699
wanna go bowling together, that's fine. Just don't discuss city business.

03:03:19.454 --> 03:03:22.296
It's not a meeting and therefore not subject to the act.

03:03:24.490 --> 03:03:27.970
Again, typically we would only discuss items that are properly

03:03:28.470 --> 03:03:32.050
noticed. There's, again, noticing requirements, there's emergency

03:03:32.550 --> 03:03:35.850
noticing requirements, all sorts of things. But you can't take final

03:03:36.350 --> 03:03:39.770
action on anything that isn't properly noticed. So if there's a discussion item and

03:03:40.270 --> 03:03:42.650
you— there's something that comes up where it says City Manager items and we talk

03:03:43.150 --> 03:03:46.610
about it, you couldn't take final action on that unless you specified what

03:03:47.110 --> 03:03:48.924
item you were going to take final action and notice that that separately.

03:03:51.538 --> 03:03:55.186
Okay. So this is an important one because this is,

03:03:55.315 --> 03:03:58.946
I think, 2 years old now since the legislature added this. I think it's an

03:03:59.446 --> 03:04:03.319
important rule, which is individuals constituting

03:04:03.819 --> 03:04:07.160
a quorum of a public body such as the city council may not act

03:04:07.660 --> 03:04:10.283
together outside a meeting in a concerted and deliberate way to predetermine an action to

03:04:10.783 --> 03:04:14.394
be taken at the meeting on a relevant matter. And relevant matter is a defined

03:04:14.894 --> 03:04:18.541
term. It's just anything within your jurisdiction. So, what that

03:04:19.041 --> 03:04:22.540
means is that if one councilmember talks to a second councilmember

03:04:22.556 --> 03:04:25.896
about a relevant matter, that's fine. They can

03:04:25.992 --> 03:04:29.012
even predetermine their action if they want. I wouldn't advise it, but that is not

03:04:29.044 --> 03:04:32.175
against the law. If they then talk to a third councilmember,

03:04:32.288 --> 03:04:34.633
even if it's not all together at one time,

03:04:35.628 --> 03:04:39.467
then that, and they predetermine the action, that would be considered a violation. And here's

03:04:39.820 --> 03:04:42.807
a good example of it. I think most of you have seen this.

03:04:43.674 --> 03:04:47.110
But I think it's hard to find Open and Public Meetings

03:04:47.610 --> 03:04:51.140
Act videos, but this is a good one, and from a— you wouldn't expect

03:04:51.157 --> 03:04:54.801
it from casino. Chairman, members of the commission,

03:04:55.717 --> 03:04:59.089
Mr. Wollstein is pleased to be here today. And when the day finally came,

03:04:59.313 --> 03:05:02.830
I was ready. I felt so confident that all I had to do was

03:05:02.926 --> 03:05:06.298
present my case. And we have documents, one of which is

03:05:06.798 --> 03:05:10.330
a report by retired FBI agents which completely absolves

03:05:10.830 --> 03:05:13.790
Mr. Rothstein from any wrongdoing. I'd like this marked, Mr. Chairman. Pardon me, Counselor,

03:05:14.290 --> 03:05:17.670
before you continue. No, I want to have this marked. This commission is prepared to

03:05:18.170 --> 03:05:21.410
act on a motion denying the Rothstein application. Deny it! Do I hear a

03:05:21.910 --> 03:05:25.130
motion seconded? Mr. Chairman. Mr. Chairman, I second the motion. Do I

03:05:25.630 --> 03:05:29.250
have a vote on the motion? Mr. Chairman. Aye. Aye. Aye.

03:05:29.750 --> 03:05:31.180
The ayes have it. This hearing is adjourned.

03:05:32.290 --> 03:05:36.890
Okay, so I show that video because it I think

03:05:37.390 --> 03:05:40.450
demonstrates the rationale behind the Open and Public Meetings Act. Here,

03:05:40.950 --> 03:05:44.830
obviously, you see this really, you know, very full table

03:05:45.330 --> 03:05:48.630
full of what appear to be case reporters. I'm not sure why they would need

03:05:49.130 --> 03:05:52.950
half a dozen case reporters filled with mostly irrelevant cases,

03:05:53.450 --> 03:05:56.710
but apparently they have that. They have a bunch of documents. They spent hours preparing

03:05:57.210 --> 03:06:00.130
for this. And before they've had any discussion on the matter, they vote and decide

03:06:00.630 --> 03:06:03.850
it. Obviously, this is not the purpose of the Open and Public Meetings Act.

03:06:04.623 --> 03:06:08.859
What we want to do is have that deliberation, let them present their case,

03:06:09.420 --> 03:06:13.335
deliberate about it openly, and then make a decision, not have it predetermined like it

03:06:13.527 --> 03:06:15.340
was, as what happened in this case.

03:06:16.351 --> 03:06:19.672
So now I'm going to go with some hypotheticals.

03:06:20.667 --> 03:06:24.453
Hypothetical 1, and I've changed these from previous presentations.

03:06:24.517 --> 03:06:27.967
So Councilmember A meets with Councilmember B

03:06:28.467 --> 03:06:31.017
to discuss a preliminary plat application coming coming before the city council on the next

03:06:31.517 --> 03:06:35.306
agenda. Councilmember A convinces Councilmember B that preliminary plat does

03:06:35.338 --> 03:06:38.728
not meet the legal requirements. They decide to vote against it at the next meeting.

03:06:38.793 --> 03:06:40.991
Is this a violation of the Open and Public Meetings Act?

03:06:45.990 --> 03:06:47.448
It's okay to answer if you know the answer.

03:06:50.309 --> 03:06:54.125
Not a violation. Why? It's just

03:06:54.625 --> 03:06:57.945
2 of them. Does not constitute a quorum. But it still does create—

03:06:58.445 --> 03:07:00.935
But what you don't want to do is predetermine an outcome. Yes.

03:07:01.647 --> 03:07:04.799
That's the key. That's where you're trying to trick us. So I

03:07:05.299 --> 03:07:08.294
would say that this is not a violation, but does somewhat go against the spirit.

03:07:08.343 --> 03:07:10.538
But it kind of borders on one that is potential.

03:07:12.528 --> 03:07:15.384
Well, this is not a violation because there's not a quorum. Now, I would not

03:07:15.884 --> 03:07:19.945
recommend that you predetermine actions. It's fine to

03:07:20.173 --> 03:07:22.810
form opinions, of Of course. You look at the preliminary plat, you think it doesn't

03:07:23.310 --> 03:07:26.790
meet the legal requirements, it's natural to form that opinion based upon review.

03:07:27.290 --> 03:07:30.850
I would expect you to form an opinion. Certainly, maybe not set in stone,

03:07:31.350 --> 03:07:34.730
but certainly form an opinion prior to the meeting, because that means you studied the

03:07:35.230 --> 03:07:38.450
issue. What, you know, even predetermining in this sense

03:07:38.950 --> 03:07:41.690
is not actually a violation, but maybe against the spirit of what you're being asked

03:07:42.190 --> 03:07:45.590
to do. Okay. Hypothetical number 2.

03:07:46.090 --> 03:07:49.176
Councilmember A speaks to the mayor about a development agreement on the next On next

03:07:49.225 --> 03:07:52.858
week's city council agenda, and they agree on several specific changes that need, sorry,

03:07:53.199 --> 03:07:57.119
there's a typo, need to be made to the agreement. The mayor contacts Councilmember

03:07:57.619 --> 03:08:01.124
B and discusses the matter. Councilmember B agrees that the changes are

03:08:01.624 --> 03:08:04.020
important and indicates that he will support them. Is this a violation of the Open

03:08:04.520 --> 03:08:05.987
and Public Meetings Act? Trick question.

03:08:07.743 --> 03:08:09.158
It's not very tricky.

03:08:10.995 --> 03:08:14.748
So it is not a violation. Again, because the

03:08:15.248 --> 03:08:18.906
mayor does not count towards the quorum, that's the principle being illustrated. Again,

03:08:19.886 --> 03:08:23.321
when you're going through agreements, if you think something needs to be changed,

03:08:23.418 --> 03:08:26.822
obviously you're the policymakers, you make those decisions,

03:08:27.512 --> 03:08:30.482
and it's okay to form an opinion on that.

03:08:30.563 --> 03:08:33.629
Obviously you should be open to reconsideration or

03:08:34.129 --> 03:08:37.804
amendment or modification of those, what changes you think need to be made, or be

03:08:38.304 --> 03:08:41.400
willing to be convinced that they don't need to be made. and discuss that in

03:08:41.900 --> 03:08:45.657
the meeting. But forming these opinions and even discussing it among 2 council

03:08:46.157 --> 03:08:50.090
members and then the mayor is not a violation of the Act. So there

03:08:50.590 --> 03:08:54.330
you go. Hypothetical 3. Council Member A speaks to Council Member B

03:08:54.830 --> 03:08:58.329
about a rezone application in front of the City Council next week. He convinces

03:08:58.361 --> 03:09:01.847
Council Member B to recommend approval. Council Member B speaks to Council Member C to

03:09:01.879 --> 03:09:05.396
get her support. Council Member C dislikes the rezone application

03:09:05.509 --> 03:09:09.024
and declines to give support. Is this a violation of the Open Public Meetings Act?

03:09:11.177 --> 03:09:14.037
Yes, because they combined A, B,

03:09:14.183 --> 03:09:17.625
and C and had a discussion by proxy.

03:09:19.240 --> 03:09:22.299
That's correct. So the fact that Councilmember— Oh, sorry.

03:09:22.959 --> 03:09:27.017
The fact that Councilmember C predetermined

03:09:27.517 --> 03:09:29.990
something different than Councilmembers A and B does not matter. Okay.

03:09:31.820 --> 03:09:35.796
So it's still a violation. Okay, hypothetical 4,

03:09:35.812 --> 03:09:39.454
same facts except that the discussion occurs via text messaging.

03:09:39.841 --> 03:09:40.679
Is this a violation?

03:09:44.796 --> 03:09:48.757
Still yes. Still yes. Okay. Text messaging doesn't change the

03:09:49.208 --> 03:09:52.718
legal landscape. It's still a violation. Okay, hypothetical 5,

03:09:52.734 --> 03:09:56.073
during an open meeting, council votes to go into closed session. During the closed session,

03:09:56.089 --> 03:09:59.528
the city council discusses potentially suing a contractor over a bridge contract issue.

03:09:59.787 --> 03:10:02.627
Councilmember A asks how this will affect other agreements with the same contractor.

03:10:03.111 --> 03:10:04.919
Is this a violation of the Open Public Meetings Act?

03:10:17.097 --> 03:10:20.744
Got it. Well, they, they can go to a closed session because they're talking

03:10:20.776 --> 03:10:24.526
about litigation, so that's acceptable. They can

03:10:25.026 --> 03:10:28.733
discuss that, and I think they can discuss the ramifications of it, is my own

03:10:28.765 --> 03:10:32.009
opinion of it, but I don't know on that second part of the question.

03:10:32.154 --> 03:10:35.751
The problem comes for me when Member A asks how this will affect other agreements

03:10:36.251 --> 03:10:40.794
with the same contractor, because those are not part of the closed session item.

03:10:41.404 --> 03:10:44.408
So my favorite answer in the law is, of course, it depends,

03:10:44.584 --> 03:10:48.310
right? So I think a narrow discussion of how potentially

03:10:48.810 --> 03:10:53.219
suing a contractor, which again would fall within the reasonably imminent litigation, because you're

03:10:53.283 --> 03:10:54.968
actively discussing, should we sue this person?

03:10:58.330 --> 03:11:02.084
You know, whether, how that will affect the total landscape with that same person,

03:11:02.132 --> 03:11:05.500
and the effects of actually instituting a lawsuit,

03:11:05.532 --> 03:11:09.156
I think if you keep it in that narrow scope, you're fine. The issue

03:11:09.656 --> 03:11:12.490
comes when you don't keep it in the narrow scope, and then you run into

03:11:12.538 --> 03:11:16.129
problems. So, it's one where you have to be very vigilant in making

03:11:16.145 --> 03:11:19.626
sure that your discussion is specific to the lawsuit,

03:11:19.931 --> 03:11:23.481
and so your discussion of those other agreements is specific to the lawsuit's

03:11:23.981 --> 03:11:27.369
impact on them. And that's why you sit in every closed session and keep us

03:11:27.562 --> 03:11:32.238
on track if we start to wander slightly. I mean, Marcus has us

03:11:32.302 --> 03:11:35.756
on a short leash. Anybody who sees me out here knows I like to enforce

03:11:35.836 --> 03:11:37.122
rules, or at least I'm willing to.

03:11:38.005 --> 03:11:42.022
Okay, hypothetical 6. Same facts as hypothetical 5.

03:11:42.102 --> 03:11:44.997
Councilmember Beas About whether the city should terminate the contract,

03:11:46.447 --> 03:11:49.724
and what contractor the city should procure as a replacement.

03:11:50.303 --> 03:11:53.741
Is this a violation? Yes. Yes,

03:11:54.095 --> 03:11:57.854
and again, this is what I talked about last one, right? We gotta keep ourselves

03:11:57.886 --> 03:12:01.204
very narrowly tailored on that discussion of those other

03:12:01.704 --> 03:12:05.155
contracts, and discussion of the lawsuit. And the second we start talking about,

03:12:05.171 --> 03:12:07.757
well, who should we get to replace them, then you're all of a sudden,

03:12:07.774 --> 03:12:11.006
you've crossed the line. So don't do that. Okay,

03:12:11.086 --> 03:12:14.797
hypothetical 7. During closed session, the city council discusses the professional

03:12:15.297 --> 03:12:18.770
competence of Company 1, whose services the city is considering using. Is this a violation

03:12:19.270 --> 03:12:20.045
of the Open and Public Meetings Act?

03:12:26.952 --> 03:12:30.543
Yes. Why? Because it's an individual, not a company.

03:12:30.720 --> 03:12:34.440
That's correct. So the— you're allowed

03:12:34.940 --> 03:12:38.315
to discuss the professional competence Competence of an individual. Individual is defined

03:12:38.815 --> 03:12:41.223
as a human being in this context. Sometimes we use the word individual in the

03:12:41.723 --> 03:12:44.565
law to not just mean human beings, but also legal entities.

03:12:45.160 --> 03:12:47.859
But in the context of the Open and Public Meetings Act, it literally means a

03:12:48.359 --> 03:12:52.069
human being. So talking about a company, unless you're talking about— unless

03:12:52.569 --> 03:12:55.668
company 1 is like an individual, say like it's

03:12:56.168 --> 03:13:00.086
a lobbyist, and they're the company, it's just one individual, then maybe you could

03:13:00.586 --> 03:13:04.263
talk about that individual's competence. But as

03:13:04.279 --> 03:13:07.993
far as, you know, you're talking about like Meta, obviously no.

03:13:08.252 --> 03:13:11.627
You're not talking about anyone's, any specific

03:13:12.127 --> 03:13:15.513
individual's confidence. You're talking about the company. So that would be a violation. Okay.

03:13:15.772 --> 03:13:19.436
So thus concludes the Open Meetings Act portion

03:13:20.663 --> 03:13:21.939
of the training. Are there any questions?

03:13:25.102 --> 03:13:28.707
No, but it's always fun to have you try and trick us, Marcus. I do

03:13:29.207 --> 03:13:32.795
what I can. Try to make it interesting,

03:13:32.909 --> 03:13:36.426
so try to make you think so it's not just rote memorization but

03:13:36.926 --> 03:13:40.831
actually critical thinking. So, alrighty, let's go to Municipal

03:13:41.331 --> 03:13:44.469
Officers and Employees Ethics Act. All right,

03:13:44.826 --> 03:13:47.995
from the beginning. Okay.

03:13:49.081 --> 03:13:53.035
So the purpose of this, of the act,

03:13:53.165 --> 03:13:56.876
is to establish standards for conduct for municipal officers and employees and to require these

03:13:56.909 --> 03:14:00.683
persons disclose actual or potential conflicts of interest between

03:14:01.183 --> 03:14:03.827
their public duties and their personal interests. Okay.

03:14:05.286 --> 03:14:08.942
So here's the first thing. It's unlawful to disclose improperly used private,

03:14:08.958 --> 03:14:13.257
controlled, or protected information acquired by reason of your official position

03:14:14.395 --> 03:14:17.635
in the course of your duties in order to further substantially your

03:14:18.421 --> 03:14:21.837
personal economic interests or to secure special privileges or exemption

03:14:21.933 --> 03:14:25.109
for you or for others. Okay. So this

03:14:25.174 --> 03:14:28.528
is pretty straightforward. In the event that you come into contact,

03:14:28.993 --> 03:14:31.866
and I don't think most of you would on any sort of regular basis,

03:14:31.882 --> 03:14:35.910
but in the event you come into contact with private control or protected records

03:14:36.410 --> 03:14:40.147
or other information that would fall under that, you can't use that for your own

03:14:40.163 --> 03:14:42.490
gain. I think this falls in the common sense category.

03:14:43.437 --> 03:14:48.026
Whenever you obtain something via your public

03:14:48.526 --> 03:14:51.237
service and you try using it for personal gain. I think common sense tells you

03:14:51.737 --> 03:14:54.689
that might be wrong. Okay.

03:14:55.395 --> 03:14:58.864
It is unlawful to— and so anytime— so I just want to clarify

03:14:59.364 --> 03:15:03.167
something. Anytime in the law we say unlawful, it means it's a crime. So sometimes

03:15:03.215 --> 03:15:07.341
you say, you know, you can violate the law and it's just there's implications

03:15:07.438 --> 03:15:10.922
where you could be liable in a civil suit. But anytime you use the word

03:15:11.422 --> 03:15:14.085
in a statute or in an ordinance that says it's unlawful, it means you're committing

03:15:14.101 --> 03:15:17.731
a crime and could be prosecuted. Okay, it is

03:15:18.231 --> 03:15:21.763
unlawful to knowingly receive, accept, take, seek, or solicit directly or indirectly

03:15:22.263 --> 03:15:25.537
for the officer or employer or for another a gift of

03:15:26.037 --> 03:15:29.424
substantial value or a substantial economic benefit tantamount to a gift that would

03:15:30.243 --> 03:15:34.275
tender improperly influence a reasonable person in your position to depart from

03:15:34.775 --> 03:15:38.290
faithful and impartial discharge of your duties, that you know or should

03:15:38.790 --> 03:15:42.365
know that under the circumstances is primarily for the purposes

03:15:42.479 --> 03:15:44.635
of rewarding you for official action taken.

03:15:46.930 --> 03:15:50.522
So either of those things. However, there is a safe harbor provision

03:15:51.022 --> 03:15:54.164
that says an occasional gift valued at under $50 is okay.

03:15:54.342 --> 03:15:57.803
So if you're at a conference and you receive

03:15:58.303 --> 03:16:01.004
a free meal from a sponsor and it's less than $50,

03:16:01.020 --> 03:16:04.318
that's presumptively okay. Again,

03:16:04.593 --> 03:16:08.225
these are— That's the safe harbor provision.

03:16:08.306 --> 03:16:11.539
So that's a presumption. It doesn't mean that

03:16:12.039 --> 03:16:15.759
because something's $51, it's necessarily a violation. That's always a case-by-case

03:16:15.824 --> 03:16:18.343
basis. The $50 is simply a safe harbor provision.

03:16:20.534 --> 03:16:23.964
So when I was younger, not so much anymore, I used to like Law and

03:16:24.464 --> 03:16:27.361
Order. When I became a lawyer, I started to dislike it, because I see all

03:16:27.861 --> 03:16:31.407
the things that are wrong with it. But when I was younger, I liked

03:16:31.907 --> 03:16:35.622
it. And there's one episode where one

03:16:36.122 --> 03:16:39.856
individual murders his brother, and his mother, who is a rather

03:16:39.872 --> 03:16:41.852
wealthy individual, a wealthy businesswoman,

03:16:43.490 --> 03:16:47.074
then essentially bribes a judge to

03:16:47.443 --> 03:16:50.577
let the surviving son, make a ruling in favor of

03:16:51.077 --> 03:16:54.435
the surviving son to get compelling evidence of

03:16:54.935 --> 03:16:57.556
his guilt suppressed, and thus the case dismissed.

03:16:58.628 --> 03:17:02.114
And so the way that she undertakes to

03:17:02.242 --> 03:17:05.567
provide this bribe is to arrange with the bank that

03:17:06.067 --> 03:17:10.049
her business banks with to provide a substantially below market rate loan for

03:17:10.549 --> 03:17:13.647
the mortgage for the judge. So she's not actually providing anything, and that

03:17:14.147 --> 03:17:17.518
is basically, if you look at the definition of substantial

03:17:18.018 --> 03:17:21.743
economic benefit tantamount to a gift, that is basically what is described,

03:17:22.401 --> 03:17:26.376
is what occurred in that. Episode. So anything

03:17:26.537 --> 03:17:29.554
where you would receive something of value,

03:17:30.006 --> 03:17:33.893
whether it's, you know, somebody is going to sell you something at below market rate

03:17:34.393 --> 03:17:38.333
because you are on the city council or an elected official, that would

03:17:38.833 --> 03:17:40.671
be problematic. You know,

03:17:41.672 --> 03:17:45.628
again, the language here is it

03:17:46.128 --> 03:17:49.180
has to be something that would tend to improperly influence you.

03:17:51.160 --> 03:17:54.866
Or that would tend to be a reward.

03:17:55.444 --> 03:17:59.519
So, you know, you get invited by a developer to some

03:18:00.019 --> 03:18:02.776
sort of event that they're hosting, not just for the city, but for many,

03:18:04.011 --> 03:18:07.926
including employees and elected officials of the city, and they're

03:18:08.426 --> 03:18:12.146
offering you benefits at that thing, then it's just a question of, is this less

03:18:12.258 --> 03:18:14.953
than $50? If it is, I'm probably under safe harbor.

03:18:15.548 --> 03:18:18.440
If it's more than that, then it's probably a bad idea to accept,

03:18:18.456 --> 03:18:21.830
because it could be construed as a reward

03:18:22.330 --> 03:18:25.783
for official action taken. Okay, so let's

03:18:26.313 --> 03:18:29.382
go to another video. Hey, Tom, check it out.

03:18:29.864 --> 03:18:33.239
Norton Construction just sent this over. Whoa, mama.

03:18:34.122 --> 03:18:37.256
I love Great Lakes wine. No, no, no, no, no.

03:18:38.525 --> 03:18:41.599
You know as well as I do that we are not allowed to accept gifts

03:18:42.099 --> 03:18:45.197
over $25 for corruption reasons. We live in a fishbowl,

03:18:45.878 --> 03:18:49.098
and the public is always watching. But you cannot enjoy this basket.

03:18:49.163 --> 03:18:51.910
I'm so sorry. And so to avoid temptation, I'm going to lock it up.

03:18:54.581 --> 03:18:58.359
Okay. So apparently the rule in Pawnee is $25,

03:18:58.618 --> 03:19:00.459
not $50, as a safe harbor provision.

03:19:01.478 --> 03:19:05.056
But so again, that's kind of illustrative of the,

03:19:05.380 --> 03:19:08.428
you know, if it's something You know, when in doubt, you know, just don't accept

03:19:08.928 --> 03:19:12.412
the gift. I mean, maybe this is a bit overkill here, but there was booze

03:19:12.524 --> 03:19:16.299
in that if you watched the episode. So probably not a good idea to accept.

03:19:17.664 --> 03:19:21.760
Ooh, hey. Okay. So, all right. It's unlawful

03:19:22.260 --> 03:19:25.888
to use or attempt to use the officer's or employee's official position to further your

03:19:26.337 --> 03:19:29.437
personal economic interests or secure special privileges for you or others.

03:19:30.257 --> 03:19:32.489
Again, this kind of goes in the common sense territory.

03:19:33.783 --> 03:19:37.019
Don't use your office to make money for you or your family

03:19:37.519 --> 03:19:39.257
or your friends. So, cut.

03:19:42.959 --> 03:19:45.960
There's a million ways to exploit your power, but have I ever given in to

03:19:46.025 --> 03:19:49.768
that temptation? No, never. I'm not that kind of politician. Official Parks

03:19:50.268 --> 03:19:53.389
and Rec business. Just need to grab a quick hot dog. Sorry about that,

03:19:53.470 --> 03:19:56.480
little girl, you can have the next one. I have a moral code and I

03:19:56.980 --> 03:20:00.314
never violate it. People look at me and they say, wow, Tom, You are so

03:20:00.395 --> 03:20:04.039
pure. You're like a brown Superman with a beard that just stands for justice

03:20:04.794 --> 03:20:08.182
and truth and the American way. Yeah, don't do

03:20:08.682 --> 03:20:12.405
that. So obviously this is, you know, a humorous representation

03:20:12.533 --> 03:20:16.563
of kind of what not to do, and it's obviously on a very small scale.

03:20:17.398 --> 03:20:20.995
But yes, we don't use our office, our position

03:20:21.107 --> 03:20:24.399
for personal gain. They call it public service

03:20:24.899 --> 03:20:28.170
for a reason, so. Okay, so these violations can be prosecuted

03:20:28.670 --> 03:20:31.563
criminally, and depending on the value of any benefit received, it could be up to

03:20:31.579 --> 03:20:35.597
a second-degree felony. Anywhere from a Class B misdemeanor to a second-degree felony depending

03:20:35.695 --> 03:20:39.369
on the value of the gift or other benefit received.

03:20:39.954 --> 03:20:42.539
So just don't do it. When in doubt,

03:20:43.570 --> 03:20:45.420
you have my number, so just call, we'll discuss.

03:20:47.417 --> 03:20:50.517
And yes. Okay, so part of the Ethics Act,

03:20:50.967 --> 03:20:54.786
In fact, I would say the majority of it is dedicated to disclosure requirements.

03:20:55.596 --> 03:21:00.930
So it is an offense, which is another term that

03:21:01.430 --> 03:21:05.196
means it's unlawful, for a city council member to receive or agree

03:21:05.696 --> 03:21:09.060
to receive compensation for assisting any person or business entity in any transaction

03:21:09.560 --> 03:21:13.159
involving the city, unless you

03:21:13.191 --> 03:21:14.998
file a sworn statement with the mayor.

03:21:17.468 --> 03:21:20.678
And follow the requirements. You give a description of what's going

03:21:20.775 --> 03:21:24.307
on, and you disclose it in an open City Council meeting.

03:21:25.360 --> 03:21:27.948
So those are the required disclosures.

03:21:28.627 --> 03:21:32.105
Under state law, that's all you're required to do. As we'll discuss later, under city

03:21:32.605 --> 03:21:36.338
law, you would also be required to recuse yourself from any action related

03:21:36.354 --> 03:21:39.868
to that. So,

03:21:39.884 --> 03:21:43.362
okay, more disclosures. A City Council member who is an officer, director,

03:21:43.426 --> 03:21:46.736
agent, or employee, or the owner of substantial interest in any business

03:21:46.800 --> 03:21:50.487
entity which is subject to the regulation of

03:21:50.536 --> 03:21:54.158
the city shall disclose the position held and the nature and

03:21:54.658 --> 03:21:58.491
value of the interest upon first becoming appointed or elected to

03:21:58.991 --> 03:22:01.695
the city council, typically elected,

03:22:02.274 --> 03:22:06.364
and anytime thereafter if any of the information

03:22:06.864 --> 03:22:10.027
changes. So disclosure is in the

03:22:10.527 --> 03:22:14.099
form of a sworn statement to the mayor and the city recorder. And the mayor

03:22:14.599 --> 03:22:16.916
provides a copy or outlines the substance of the disclosure to the remainder of the

03:22:17.416 --> 03:22:21.247
city council. Okay, an interest of $5,000 or less need not be disclosed,

03:22:21.263 --> 03:22:24.963
and that's out of the statute. A city

03:22:25.463 --> 03:22:28.109
council member who is an officer, director, agent, employee, or owner of substantial interest in

03:22:28.609 --> 03:22:31.997
a business entity which does or anticipates doing business with the city shall publicly

03:22:32.497 --> 03:22:36.151
disclose to the members of the city council immediately prior to any discussion of any—

03:22:36.750 --> 03:22:40.352
by such body concerning matters related to that entity. And the nature

03:22:40.852 --> 03:22:44.329
of the interest. So again, so there's the disclosure you give to the mayor in

03:22:44.345 --> 03:22:47.903
writing, and then hey, now it's got business in city

03:22:48.403 --> 03:22:51.831
council on the agenda. You have to disclose it immediately before the item is called

03:22:52.331 --> 03:22:56.356
on the agenda. And you know, again, under city code, you would also recuse yourself.

03:22:59.000 --> 03:23:02.991
So violation of the disclosure requirements could lead to expulsion

03:23:03.803 --> 03:23:07.555
under the statute if they were proven would lead to expulsion. And it

03:23:08.055 --> 03:23:11.389
also makes any action in violation of the disclosure requirements voidable,

03:23:12.448 --> 03:23:15.914
which is important distinction under the law. Void and voidable mean

03:23:15.962 --> 03:23:18.786
2 different things. Void means it never took effect.

03:23:19.251 --> 03:23:22.524
Voidable means if you do certain things after the fact, you can

03:23:22.877 --> 03:23:26.856
void the action. So you can void it. It's not necessarily

03:23:27.048 --> 03:23:27.754
per se voided.

03:23:28.636 --> 03:23:31.877
Okay. All right.

03:23:31.958 --> 03:23:35.343
City code. Each public official who is a member of the city council shall

03:23:35.843 --> 03:23:39.180
disclose disclose every conflict prior to consideration of any

03:23:39.261 --> 03:23:39.552
action.

03:23:42.297 --> 03:23:45.706
And again, also for purposes of

03:23:46.206 --> 03:23:48.775
this chapter, the official has a conflict of interest if they know or have reason

03:23:49.275 --> 03:23:52.888
to know that a parent, spouse, sibling, or member

03:23:53.388 --> 03:23:56.562
of the same household, agent, principal, client, employer, or employee also would

03:23:56.594 --> 03:24:00.108
violate any of these things or would meet any of these requirements. So if you're

03:24:00.608 --> 03:24:03.740
required to disclose, Under the state act,

03:24:04.543 --> 03:24:08.011
you have to both disclose and then recuse. If you have an ownership interest

03:24:08.511 --> 03:24:11.801
in a business having matters under proper consideration for the city council, same.

03:24:13.278 --> 03:24:16.746
All these things would involve disclosure and then for an agenda item

03:24:16.810 --> 03:24:20.214
recusal. Have an ownership interest in property within

03:24:20.714 --> 03:24:24.710
Eagle Mountain City for which zoning, conditional use, or development

03:24:25.210 --> 03:24:29.750
approvals are under consideration. This would not

03:24:30.250 --> 03:24:33.745
apply to a principal residence. So if you own

03:24:34.245 --> 03:24:38.622
a house, you live in it, that would not apply. But otherwise,

03:24:38.718 --> 03:24:42.007
yes. Represents as an agent,

03:24:42.023 --> 03:24:46.050
professional services contractor, or other advisor individual with matters under consideration

03:24:46.550 --> 03:24:49.901
before the City Council. Again, if in your day job you're doing something to that

03:24:50.401 --> 03:24:53.597
effect, for example, say you worked for LRB,

03:24:53.903 --> 03:24:57.014
there are municipal financial advisors.

03:24:57.836 --> 03:25:01.463
When we're doing municipal financial stuff, then you would have to recuse from that.

03:25:05.537 --> 03:25:09.419
Okay, so has an ownership interest in business or property

03:25:09.436 --> 03:25:11.781
that has been issued a business license by Eagle Mountain,

03:25:13.236 --> 03:25:16.780
uses or develops property located within the city limits

03:25:16.942 --> 03:25:21.328
other than your personal residence, And which offers

03:25:21.360 --> 03:25:24.877
the same goods, products, or services, or would use or develop property in a similar

03:25:25.377 --> 03:25:28.987
way as a business or property which has matter. So again, this is related to,

03:25:30.272 --> 03:25:33.932
you have, you know, you own property and you're a competitor to

03:25:33.948 --> 03:25:37.385
the person that's being, that's getting

03:25:37.497 --> 03:25:41.495
rezoned. So you would have a conflict there because they're

03:25:41.575 --> 03:25:43.037
asking for a rezone and you're their competitor.

03:25:48.447 --> 03:25:51.987
Again, this relates to zoning, conditional use, or development approvals.

03:25:52.036 --> 03:25:55.075
If you own property that's gonna be affected indirectly or directly,

03:25:57.670 --> 03:26:00.511
other than, again, other than a primary residence, then you would have to disclose and

03:26:00.544 --> 03:26:04.230
recuse. If you have agreement with party seeking approval

03:26:04.279 --> 03:26:06.067
that would finally financially benefit you,

03:26:07.600 --> 03:26:11.015
then same thing. So hypothetical 1. Okay, City Councilmember

03:26:11.112 --> 03:26:14.782
A attends a conference to receive training on his duties. Company 1 invites

03:26:15.282 --> 03:26:18.260
City Councilmember A to dinner and pays. Company 1 has business before the City Council.

03:26:18.308 --> 03:26:21.879
The cost of the dinner is $40. Is this a violation of

03:26:22.379 --> 03:26:22.527
the Ethics Act?

03:26:26.375 --> 03:26:29.446
No, because it's under $50. That's correct.

03:26:29.770 --> 03:26:32.373
Safe harbor provision, you know,

03:26:32.715 --> 03:26:36.243
again, the standard is if it would you know,

03:26:36.324 --> 03:26:38.262
reasonable person would, you know,

03:26:39.715 --> 03:26:43.446
would be, I guess, influenced to

03:26:43.946 --> 03:26:47.493
not impartially discharge their duties. However, if it's under $50, there's the

03:26:47.993 --> 03:26:50.960
presumption that it's fine. Okay.

03:26:51.565 --> 03:26:54.264
Dinner costs $75. Otherwise, it's the same hypothetical.

03:26:56.252 --> 03:26:58.710
Is this a violation? I would say probably not.

03:27:00.423 --> 03:27:03.878
It's outside the safe harbor provision. Would a $75 meal cause

03:27:03.894 --> 03:27:08.678
you to change your vote? So I would assume not. But again,

03:27:08.935 --> 03:27:11.998
at $51, is that a per se violation? No,

03:27:12.800 --> 03:27:15.927
it's probably not a violation. At a $10,000

03:27:16.427 --> 03:27:19.856
meal, they take you to some White House fundraiser dinner, it's probably

03:27:19.920 --> 03:27:23.239
a violation. Where you draw the line in between those 2 numbers is unclear.

03:27:23.705 --> 03:27:26.495
So my advice is always just keep it under $50. You don't have a problem.

03:27:27.698 --> 03:27:31.001
At $75, is it not a per se violation? But it could be.

